var e={level:`B1`,type:`multiple-choice`,questions:[{id:`b1-mc-001`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I have lived in Rome ___ 2015.`,explanation:`Use 'since' with a point in time (2015) and 'for' with a period of time (ten years).`,wrongReasons:{0:`'for' is used with a length of time, such as 'for ten years'.`,2:`'from' needs 'to' to show a period.`,3:`'during' is followed by an event or period, not a starting year.`},options:[`for`,`since`,`from`,`during`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-003`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`Have you ever ___ sushi?`,explanation:`Present perfect: have/has + past participle. Example: Have you ever eaten Thai food?`,wrongReasons:{0:`'ate' is the past simple; after 'have' you need the past participle.`,1:`'eat' is the base form; the present perfect needs the past participle.`,2:`'eating' is the -ing form; it does not follow 'have' in the present perfect.`},options:[`ate`,`eat`,`eating`,`eaten`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-007`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`You ___ wear a seatbelt in a car. It's the law.`,explanation:`Use 'have to' for rules and obligations. Example: You have to show your passport.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'might' shows possibility, not a legal rule.`,2:`'don't have to' means it isn't necessary, but the law says it is.`,3:`'could' shows possibility or ability, not obligation.`},options:[`have to`,`might`,`don't have to`,`could`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-008`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`When I was a child, I ___ play in the street every day.`,explanation:`Use 'used to' + base verb for past habits that are finished. Example: I used to walk to school.`,wrongReasons:{0:`In positive sentences, write 'used to', with -d.`,1:`'was used to' means 'was familiar with' and is followed by -ing or a noun.`,3:`'am used to' is present and means 'am familiar with'.`},options:[`use to`,`was used to`,`used to`,`am used to`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-009`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`My brother hasn't finished his homework ___.`,explanation:`Use 'yet' at the end of negative sentences and questions. Example: She hasn't called yet.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'already' is used in positive sentences, not with 'hasn't'.`,1:`'still' goes before the negative verb: 'He still hasn't finished'.`,2:`'ever' is used in questions and before the participle, not at the end.`},options:[`already`,`still`,`ever`,`yet`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-011`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If I see Maria, I ___ her your message.`,explanation:`First conditional: if + present simple, will + base form. Example: If I see him, I'll tell him.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present continuous is not used in the result of a first conditional.`,1:`'would give' is for imaginary situations, not a real future possibility.`,3:`The past simple does not talk about the future.`},options:[`am giving`,`would give`,`will give`,`gave`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-015`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`We have known each other ___ ten years.`,explanation:`Use 'for' with a length of time. Example: I have worked here for six months.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'since' needs a starting point, like 'since 2016', not a length of time.`,2:`'ago' is used with the past simple: 'ten years ago'.`,3:`'during' is followed by an event, like 'during the war', not by a length of time.`},options:[`for`,`since`,`ago`,`during`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-016`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`What ___ you doing at 8 o'clock last night?`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for an action in progress at a time in the past. Example: What were you doing at noon?`,wrongReasons:{1:`'did' is followed by the base form, not by 'doing'.`,2:`'are' is present, but the question is about last night.`,3:`'was' is used with I/he/she/it, not with 'you'.`},options:[`were`,`did`,`are`,`was`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-017`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`We'll miss the bus if we ___ now.`,explanation:`In the if-clause, use the present simple, even for the future. Example: If you don't hurry, you'll be late.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'if' in a first conditional, use the present simple, not 'will'.`,1:`The past simple does not fit with 'we'll miss'.`,3:`A negative present verb needs 'don't'.`},options:[`won't leave`,`didn't leave`,`don't leave`,`not leave`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-020`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`My dad ___ smoke, but he stopped ten years ago.`,explanation:`Use 'used to' for a habit in the past that has stopped. Example: He used to drink a lot of coffee.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'used to' has no present form like 'uses to'.`,1:`'is used to' means 'is familiar with' and is followed by -ing.`,3:`'was using to' is not a correct form.`},options:[`uses to`,`is used to`,`used to`,`was using to`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-021`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I ___ this film three times, so I know the ending.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect for experiences with a number of times. Example: She has read that book twice.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'see' meaning watch with your eyes is not normally used in the continuous form here.`,1:`The present simple does not talk about experiences up to now.`,2:`The past continuous describes an action in progress, not a number of times.`},options:[`am seeing`,`see`,`was seeing`,`have seen`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-022`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`The children were playing outside ___ their mother called them in.`,explanation:`Use 'when' before a short action that interrupts a longer one. Example: I was driving when I saw him.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'while' is used before a longer action, not a short one like 'called'.`,2:`'during' is followed by a noun, not by a clause.`,3:`'as long as' means 'if', which does not fit here.`},options:[`while`,`when`,`during`,`as long as`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-023`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`___ you need help, call me.`,explanation:`Use 'if' for a possible situation. Example: If you are hungry, eat something.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Unless' means 'if not', so the meaning would be wrong.`,2:`'Because' gives a reason, but this sentence is about a possible situation.`,3:`'Although' shows contrast, which does not fit here.`},options:[`Unless`,`If`,`Because`,`Although`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-025`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`You ___ smoke in the hospital. It's not allowed.`,explanation:`Use 'mustn't' when something is not allowed. Example: You mustn't park here.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'don't have to' means it is not necessary, not that it is forbidden.`,2:`'might not' shows uncertainty, not a rule.`,3:`'needn't' means it is not necessary, not that it is forbidden.`},options:[`don't have to`,`mustn't`,`might not`,`needn't`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-026`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`There ___ be a cinema here, but now it's a supermarket.`,explanation:`Use 'there used to be' for something that existed in the past but not now. Example: There used to be a park here.`,wrongReasons:{0:`In positive sentences, write 'used to', with -d.`,1:`'was used' is the passive of 'use' and does not fit before 'be'.`,3:`'uses to' is not a correct form.`},options:[`use to`,`was used`,`used to`,`uses to`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-027`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`___ you finished the report yet?`,explanation:`Present perfect questions: Have/Has + subject + past participle. Example: Have you eaten yet?`,wrongReasons:{1:`'has' is used with he/she/it, not with 'you'.`,2:`'are' does not form the present perfect; you need 'have'.`,3:`'do' is followed by the base form, not by 'finished'.`},options:[`Have`,`Has`,`Are`,`Do`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-028`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`She hurt her hand while she ___ the vegetables.`,explanation:`After 'while', use the past continuous for the longer background action. Example: He fell while he was running.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present continuous does not fit with the past 'hurt'.`,1:`The present perfect does not describe the background action in the past.`,2:`The present simple does not fit in a story about the past.`},options:[`is cutting`,`has cut`,`cuts`,`was cutting`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-029`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`You won't pass the exam ___ you study harder.`,explanation:`'Unless' means 'if not'. Example: I won't go unless you come with me.`,wrongReasons:{1:`With 'if', the sentence means studying harder makes you fail.`,2:`'when' means it will surely happen, which gives the wrong meaning.`,3:`'so' shows a result, not a condition.`},options:[`unless`,`if`,`when`,`so`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-030`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The letters ___ every morning at nine.`,explanation:`Present passive for regular actions: am/is/are + past participle. Example: The rooms are cleaned every day.`,wrongReasons:{1:`Letters do not deliver; someone delivers them, so use the passive.`,2:`'letters' is plural, so you need 'are'.`,3:`This is not a full verb; you need 'are delivered'.`},options:[`are delivered`,`deliver`,`is delivered`,`delivering`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-032`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I ___ like vegetables, but now I love them.`,explanation:`Negative: didn't use to + base verb. Example: I didn't use to drink tea.`,wrongReasons:{1:`After 'didn't', write 'use to' without -d.`,2:`A negative needs 'didn't': 'didn't use to'.`,3:`'used to' is only past, so 'don't use to' is wrong.`},options:[`didn't use to`,`didn't used to`,`not used to`,`don't use to`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-033`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`The train has ___ left. You've missed it.`,explanation:`Use 'already' between has/have and the participle for something that happened sooner than expected. Example: I've already paid.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'yet' goes at the end of negative sentences and questions.`,2:`'ever' is used in questions about experience, not here.`,3:`'since' needs a point in time after it.`},options:[`yet`,`already`,`ever`,`since`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-034`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`At midnight, most people in the village ___.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for an action in progress at a specific past time. Example: At ten, I was watching TV.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present continuous does not fit a story about the past.`,2:`The present perfect does not show an action in progress at a past time.`,3:`The present simple talks about habits, not one moment in the past.`},options:[`are sleeping`,`were sleeping`,`have slept`,`sleep`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-037`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`When I was five, I ___ swim.`,explanation:`Use 'could' for ability in the past. Example: She could read when she was four.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'can' is present; 'When I was five' is past.`,2:`'must' shows obligation, not past ability.`,3:`'should' gives advice, not past ability.`},options:[`can`,`could`,`must`,`should`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-038`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`She ___ walk to school, but now she takes the bus.`,explanation:`Use 'used to' for a past habit compared with the present. Example: We used to cycle, but now we drive.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The present simple does not show a finished past habit.`,2:`In positive sentences, write 'used to', with -d.`,3:`'was use to' is not a correct form.`},options:[`used to`,`uses`,`use to`,`was use to`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-039`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`How long ___ in this flat? – Since March.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect with 'how long' for a situation that started in the past and continues now. Example: How long have you had your car?`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple cannot be used with 'how long' for a time up to now.`,1:`The present continuous cannot show the time from March until now.`,2:`The past simple is for a finished time; 'since March' goes up to now.`},options:[`do you live`,`are you living`,`did you live`,`have you lived`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-040`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I saw an accident while I ___ to work this morning.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for the background action. Example: I met Ana while I was waiting for the bus.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The past perfect means the drive was finished before you saw it.`,1:`The present continuous does not fit with 'saw' and 'this morning'.`,2:`The present perfect is not used for a background action in a past story.`},options:[`had driven`,`am driving`,`have driven`,`was driving`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-041`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If it ___ sunny on Saturday, we'll have a picnic.`,explanation:`First conditional: if + present simple, will + verb. Example: If it is cold, I'll wear a coat.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'if' in a first conditional, use the present simple, not 'will'.`,1:`'would' is not used after 'if' in a first conditional.`,2:`The past simple does not fit with 'we'll have'.`},options:[`will be`,`would be`,`was`,`is`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-042`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The windows ___ once a month.`,explanation:`Present passive for routines: are + past participle. Example: The floors are washed every week.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Windows do not clean themselves; use the passive.`,1:`This is active continuous; windows are not doing the action.`,3:`Without 'are', this is not a correct present passive.`},options:[`clean`,`are cleaning`,`are cleaned`,`cleaned`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-043`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`___ I open the window? It's very hot.`,explanation:`Use 'Can I...?' to ask for permission. Example: Can I use your pen?`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Am' is not followed by a base verb like 'open'.`,2:`'Have' is not followed by a base verb like 'open' in a question.`,3:`'Does' is used with he/she/it, not with 'I'.`},options:[`Am`,`Can`,`Have`,`Does`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-044`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`Where ___ to live before you moved here?`,explanation:`Questions: (Wh-) + did + subject + use to + verb? Example: What did you use to eat for breakfast?`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'did', write 'use' without -d.`,1:`Questions with 'used to' need 'did'.`,3:`'were' does not form questions with 'use to'.`},options:[`did you used`,`used you`,`did you use`,`were you use`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-045`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I have never ___ to Japan, but I'd love to go.`,explanation:`To talk about visiting a place, use 'have been to'. Example: She has never been to Africa.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'being' is the -ing form; after 'have' you need the past participle.`,2:`'went' is the past simple, not the past participle.`,3:`'go' is the base form; the present perfect needs a participle.`},options:[`been`,`being`,`went`,`go`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-046`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`When the teacher came in, the students ___ quiet immediately.`,explanation:`For one action quickly after another, use the past simple for both. Example: When the bell rang, everyone left.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'immediately' shows a quick action, so the continuous does not fit.`,1:`'become' is the base form or present, not past.`,3:`The present perfect does not fit in a past story with 'came'.`},options:[`were becoming`,`become`,`became`,`have become`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-047`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`What will you do if you ___ the job?`,explanation:`First conditional questions: What will you do if + present simple? Example: What will you do if it rains?`,wrongReasons:{0:`Don't use 'will' in the if-clause of a first conditional.`,2:`The past simple does not match 'What will you do'.`,3:`A negative present verb needs 'don't'.`},options:[`won't get`,`don't get`,`didn't get`,`not get`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-048`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`My car ___ yesterday, so I took the bus.`,explanation:`Past passive: was + past participle when we don't know who did it. Example: My bike was stolen last week.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The car did not steal anything; you need the passive.`,2:`'yesterday' is past, so the present passive is wrong.`,3:`This is active and the present perfect does not go with 'yesterday'.`},options:[`was stolen`,`stole`,`is stolen`,`has stolen`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-049`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Students ___ use their phones during the exam. It's against the rules.`,explanation:`Use 'mustn't' for something against the rules. Example: You mustn't run by the pool.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'don't have to' means it is not necessary, not that it is forbidden.`,1:`'should' gives advice to do it, but it's against the rules.`,3:`'can' means it is allowed, but the rules say it isn't.`},options:[`don't have to`,`should`,`mustn't`,`can`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-050`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`We ___ go camping every summer when we were kids.`,explanation:`Use 'used to' for repeated actions in the past. Example: They used to visit us every Sunday.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'are used to' is present and means 'are familiar with'.`,1:`The present simple does not fit with 'when we were kids'.`,3:`'were used' is the passive of 'use' and does not fit here.`},options:[`are used to`,`use`,`used to`,`were used`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-051`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`She has worked at the hospital ___ she finished university.`,explanation:`Use 'since' + a point in time or a past event. Example: I've been happy since I moved here.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'for' is followed by a length of time, not by a clause with a past event.`,1:`'ago' comes after a length of time and is used with the past simple.`,2:`'from' is not used alone to join two clauses like this.`},options:[`for`,`ago`,`from`,`since`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-052`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`___ it snowing when you left the house?`,explanation:`Past continuous question: was/were + subject + -ing. Example: Was it raining when you arrived?`,wrongReasons:{0:`'did' is followed by the base form, not 'snowing'.`,1:`'is' is present, but 'left' is past.`,2:`'were' is used with you/we/they, not with 'it'.`},options:[`Did`,`Is`,`Were`,`Was`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-054`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`Football ___ all over the world.`,explanation:`Present passive for general facts: is + past participle. Example: Tea is drunk in many countries.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Football does not play; people play it, so use the passive.`,2:`This is active continuous; football is not doing the action.`,3:`Without 'is', this is not a correct present passive.`},options:[`plays`,`is played`,`is playing`,`played`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-055`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`She isn't answering her phone. She ___ be busy.`,explanation:`Use 'might' to say something is possibly true. Example: He might be at home.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'has' is not followed by a base verb like 'be'; you would need 'has to'.`,1:`'mustn't' means something is forbidden, not a guess.`,2:`'shouldn't' gives advice, not a guess about a situation.`},options:[`has`,`mustn't`,`shouldn't`,`might`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-056`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`Our town ___ have so many cars. It was much quieter.`,explanation:`Negative past habits or states: didn't use to + verb. Example: My street didn't use to be so busy.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'used to' is only past; there is no 'doesn't use to'.`,2:`'wasn't' cannot form the negative of 'used to'.`,3:`After 'didn't', the verb must be 'use' without -d.`},options:[`doesn't use to`,`didn't use to`,`wasn't use to`,`didn't used to`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-057`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`This is the best pizza I ___ ever eaten.`,explanation:`After a superlative, use the present perfect with 'ever'. Example: It's the funniest show I've ever seen.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'did' is not used with a past participle like 'eaten'.`,2:`'am' does not form the present perfect.`,3:`'has' is used with he/she/it, not with 'I'.`},options:[`did`,`have`,`am`,`has`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-058`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`We weren't watching TV; we ___ a board game.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous to describe what was in progress at a past moment. Example: She wasn't working; she was resting.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple does not fit with 'weren't watching'.`,2:`The present perfect does not describe what was in progress at that moment.`,3:`The present continuous does not match the past 'weren't'.`},options:[`play`,`were playing`,`have played`,`are playing`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-059`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`I'll lend you my laptop if you ___ careful with it.`,explanation:`First conditional: will + verb, if + present simple. Example: I'll help you if you are free.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Don't use 'will' after 'if' in a first conditional.`,1:`'were' is for imaginary situations, but this is a real offer.`,2:`'be' is the base form; you need 'are' with 'you'.`},options:[`will be`,`were`,`be`,`are`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-060`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The film ___ by millions of people last year.`,explanation:`Past passive: was/were + past participle (+ by). Example: The game was seen by many fans.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The film did not watch anything; use the passive 'was watched'.`,2:`'last year' is past, so the present passive is wrong.`,3:`This is active and the present perfect does not go with 'last year'.`},options:[`was watched`,`watched`,`is watched`,`has watched`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-061`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Before the accident, Sam ___ run very fast.`,explanation:`Use 'could' for an ability someone had in the past. Example: My grandmother could sing beautifully.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'can' is present; 'Before the accident' is past.`,1:`'should' gives advice, not past ability.`,2:`'has to' shows present obligation, not past ability.`},options:[`can`,`should`,`has to`,`could`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-062`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`People ___ write letters much more before email.`,explanation:`Use 'used to' for things that were common in the past. Example: People used to listen to the radio more.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'were used to' means 'were familiar with' and is followed by -ing.`,1:`In positive sentences, write 'used to', with -d.`,2:`'are using to' is not a correct form.`},options:[`were used to`,`use to`,`are using to`,`used to`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-063`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I ___ my grandparents since Christmas.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect with 'since' for a period up to now. Example: I haven't eaten since breakfast.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The past simple cannot be used with 'since' for a time up to now.`,2:`The present simple does not show a period from Christmas until now.`,3:`The past continuous is for an action in progress in the past.`},options:[`haven't seen`,`didn't see`,`don't see`,`wasn't seeing`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-064`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`The lights went out while the doctor ___ the patient.`,explanation:`Use while + past continuous for an action in progress when something else happened. Example: The phone rang while I was cooking.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The present simple does not fit with the past 'went out'.`,2:`The present perfect does not show an action in progress in the past.`,3:`The present continuous is for now, not for a past story.`},options:[`was examining`,`examines`,`has examined`,`is examining`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-065`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If my phone battery dies, I ___ call you.`,explanation:`A modal like 'can' can be used in the result of a first conditional. Example: If it rains, we can stay inside.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'couldn't' is past or imaginary, but this is a real future situation.`,2:`'can' has no 'will' form; say 'won't be able to'.`,3:`The negative of 'can' is 'can't' or 'cannot'.`},options:[`couldn't`,`can't`,`won't can`,`not can`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-066`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`Our homework ___ by the teacher every Friday.`,explanation:`Present passive + by for who does it. Example: The bills are paid by my father.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Homework does not check anything; use the passive.`,2:`Without 'is', this is not a correct present passive.`,3:`This is active continuous; the homework is not doing the action.`},options:[`checks`,`is checked`,`checked`,`is checking`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-067`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`In my job, I ___ wear a uniform every day.`,explanation:`Use 'have to' (I/you/we/they) and 'has to' (he/she/it) for obligation. Example: We have to start at eight.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'has to' is for he/she/it, not 'I'.`,1:`'must' is followed by the base form without 'to'.`,2:`'having to' is not a full verb here.`},options:[`has to`,`must to`,`having to`,`have to`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-068`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`My sister ___ be very shy, but now she talks to everyone.`,explanation:`Use 'used to' + be for past states that changed. Example: He used to be thin.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Without 'did', the form is 'used to', with -d.`,1:`'is used to' is present and means 'is familiar with'.`,2:`'was used' is passive and does not fit before 'be'.`},options:[`use to`,`is used to`,`was used`,`used to`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-069`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`They moved to Canada two years ___.`,explanation:`Use 'ago' with the past simple for a finished time. Example: I started school ten years ago.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'since' needs a point in time, and goes with the present perfect.`,1:`'for' would need a different verb, like 'They lived there for two years'.`,3:`'already' does not follow a length of time like 'two years'.`},options:[`since`,`for`,`ago`,`already`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-070`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`He ___ his phone in the taxi and never saw it again.`,explanation:`Use the past simple for single, finished actions in a story. Example: She lost her bag and called the police.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Leaving the phone is a single, short action, so the continuous does not fit.`,1:`The present perfect does not go with 'never saw' in a finished story.`,3:`The present simple does not fit with the past 'saw'.`},options:[`was leaving`,`has left`,`left`,`leaves`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-071`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If the shop is closed, we ___ to the market instead.`,explanation:`First conditional: if + present simple, will + verb. Example: If the bus is late, I will walk.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present perfect does not talk about a future result.`,1:`The past simple does not fit with 'is closed'.`,3:`'would go' is for imaginary situations with a past verb after 'if'.`},options:[`have gone`,`went`,`will go`,`would go`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-072`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`These cakes ___ by my grandmother this morning.`,explanation:`Past passive with plural subjects: were + past participle. Example: The photos were taken by Sam.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The cakes did not make anything; use the passive.`,2:`'this morning' is a finished past time here, so use the past passive.`,3:`'cakes' is plural, so you need 'were'.`},options:[`made`,`were made`,`are made`,`was made`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-073`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`You ___ see the new science museum. It's amazing!`,explanation:`Use 'should' to recommend something. Example: You should try this cake.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'mustn't' means it is not allowed, but the speaker loves the museum.`,2:`'don't have to' means it isn't necessary, which is not a recommendation.`,3:`'shouldn't' advises against it, but the museum is 'amazing'.`},options:[`mustn't`,`should`,`don't have to`,`shouldn't`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-074`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`___ your grandparents use to live on a farm?`,explanation:`Questions with 'used to' start with 'Did'. Example: Did you use to play tennis?`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Were' does not form questions with 'use to'.`,1:`'Have' does not form questions with 'use to'.`,2:`'used to' is about the past, so you need 'Did', not 'Do'.`},options:[`Were`,`Have`,`Do`,`Did`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-075`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`Have you ___ been to a classical music concert?`,explanation:`Use 'ever' in questions about experience in your life. Example: Have you ever flown in a plane?`,wrongReasons:{0:`'yet' goes at the end of the question, not before the participle.`,2:`'already' asks if something happened sooner than expected, not about life experience.`,3:`'since' needs a point in time after it.`},options:[`yet`,`ever`,`already`,`since`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-076`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`When I arrived at the party, my friends ___. Everyone was on the dance floor.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous to describe a scene already in progress. Example: When I got home, my sister was singing.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'danced' would mean they started dancing after you arrived.`,2:`The present perfect does not fit a past story.`,3:`The present simple describes habits, not a past scene.`},options:[`danced`,`were dancing`,`have danced`,`dance`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-078`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`Two people ___ in the accident yesterday.`,explanation:`Past passive: were + past participle; 'hurt' stays 'hurt'. Example: Nobody was hurt in the fire.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'yesterday' is past, so the present passive is wrong.`,2:`'two people' is plural, so you need 'were'.`,3:`'hurt' is irregular; the past participle is 'hurt', not 'hurted'.`},options:[`were hurt`,`are hurt`,`was hurt`,`were hurted`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-079`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`___ you help me carry this box, please?`,explanation:`Use 'Could you...?' for polite requests. Example: Could you close the door, please?`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Should' asks for advice, not for help.`,1:`'Must' talks about obligation, not a polite request.`,2:`'Might' is not used to ask someone to do something.`},options:[`Should`,`Must`,`Might`,`Could`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-080`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I used to ___ football every weekend.`,explanation:`After 'used to', always use the base form of the verb. Example: She used to dance.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'used to', use the base form, not -ing.`,2:`After 'used to', use the base form, not the past simple.`,3:`After 'used to', use the base form without -s.`},options:[`playing`,`play`,`played`,`plays`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-081`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`My parents ___ married for thirty years, and they are still happy together.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect + for for a state that started in the past and continues. Example: We have been friends for years.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The present simple cannot be used with 'for thirty years' up to now.`,2:`The past simple means the marriage ended, but they are still together.`,3:`'has' is for he/she/it; 'my parents' is plural.`},options:[`have been`,`are`,`were`,`has been`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-082`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`The sun was shining and the birds ___ in the trees.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous to set the scene in a story. Example: It was raining and people were running.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple does not match 'was shining'.`,2:`The present continuous is for now, not a past scene.`,3:`The present perfect does not describe a background scene in the past.`},options:[`sing`,`were singing`,`are singing`,`have sung`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-083`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Call me ___ you get home.`,explanation:`Use 'when' + present simple for something you are sure will happen. Example: Text me when you arrive.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'unless' means 'if not', which makes no sense here.`,2:`'while' is for a longer action, not a moment like arriving home.`,3:`'during' is followed by a noun, not a clause.`},options:[`when`,`unless`,`while`,`during`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-084`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`When ___ the castle built?`,explanation:`Past passive questions: When/Where + was/were + subject + past participle? Example: When was this house built?`,wrongReasons:{1:`'did' is used for active questions; the passive needs 'was'.`,2:`The castle was built in the past, so 'is' is wrong.`,3:`'has' does not form a past passive question.`},options:[`was`,`did`,`is`,`has`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-085`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`I'm not sure, but we ___ go to the beach this weekend.`,explanation:`Use 'might' when you are not sure about a plan. Example: I might visit my aunt on Sunday.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'must' shows strong obligation, but the speaker is 'not sure'.`,1:`'have to' shows necessity, not uncertainty.`,2:`'mustn't' means it is forbidden, not uncertain.`},options:[`must`,`have to`,`mustn't`,`might`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-086`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`Before smartphones, we used to ___ paper maps when we travelled.`,explanation:`Used to + base form for past habits. Example: We used to buy CDs.`,wrongReasons:{1:`After 'used to', the verb is in the base form, not -ing.`,2:`After 'used to', the verb is in the base form, not the past.`,3:`After 'used to', the verb does not take -s.`},options:[`use`,`using`,`used`,`uses`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-087`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`We ___ to the new museum yet.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect with 'yet' in negative sentences. Example: I haven't been to the new café yet.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The present simple does not go with 'yet'.`,2:`'aren't' is the present of 'be' and does not talk about visits up to now.`,3:`'wasn't' is singular and the past continuous does not go with 'yet'.`},options:[`haven't been`,`don't go`,`aren't`,`wasn't going`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-088`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I didn't hear you because I ___ to music.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for an activity in progress at a past moment. Example: I missed your call because I was driving.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present perfect does not explain what was in progress at that moment.`,1:`The present continuous does not match the past 'didn't hear'.`,3:`The present simple does not fit with the past 'didn't hear'.`},options:[`have listened`,`am listening`,`was listening`,`listen`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-089`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If people don't drink enough water, they ___ tired.`,explanation:`Zero conditional: if + present simple, present simple for things that are always true. Example: If you don't sleep, you feel tired.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The past simple does not fit a general truth.`,1:`'would' is for imaginary situations, not general facts.`,3:`The past perfect does not fit a general truth.`},options:[`got`,`would get`,`get`,`had got`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-090`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`Credit cards ___ in this shop.`,explanation:`Present passive with a plural subject: are + past participle. Example: Dogs are not allowed here.`,wrongReasons:{1:`Credit cards do not accept anything; use the passive.`,2:`This is active continuous; the cards are not doing the action.`,3:`'credit cards' is plural, so you need 'are'.`},options:[`are accepted`,`accept`,`are accepting`,`is accepted`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-091`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Did you ___ work late yesterday?`,explanation:`For past obligation use 'had to'; in questions: Did + subject + have to? Example: Did she have to wait long?`,wrongReasons:{0:`'must' has no past form and cannot follow 'did'.`,1:`After 'did', use the base form 'have to', not 'had to'.`,3:`'should' cannot follow 'did'.`},options:[`must`,`had to`,`have to`,`should`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-092`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`He ___ to be a teacher before he became a writer.`,explanation:`Use 'used to be' for a past job or state. Example: She used to be a nurse.`,wrongReasons:{1:`In positive sentences, write 'used', with -d.`,2:`'used to' has no present form like 'uses to'.`,3:`'was used to be' is not correct; say 'used to be'.`},options:[`used`,`use`,`uses`,`was used`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-094`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`My dad was reading the newspaper while my mum ___ the garden.`,explanation:`Use while + past continuous for two long actions at the same time. Example: I was studying while he was cooking.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple does not match 'was reading'.`,1:`The present perfect does not show two actions happening at the same time in the past.`,2:`The present continuous is for now, not for the past.`},options:[`waters`,`has watered`,`is watering`,`was watering`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-095`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If I ___ time this evening, I'll call my grandmother.`,explanation:`In the first conditional, the if-clause uses the present simple. Example: If I have money, I'll buy it.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Don't use 'will' in the if-clause.`,1:`The past simple does not fit with 'I'll call'.`,2:`'would' is not used after 'if' in a first conditional.`},options:[`will have`,`had`,`would have`,`have`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-096`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The new school ___ by the mayor last week.`,explanation:`Past passive + by: was + past participle. Example: The hospital was opened by the queen.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The school did not open anything; with 'by the mayor' you need the passive.`,2:`'last week' is past, so the present passive is wrong.`,3:`This is active and the present perfect does not go with 'last week'.`},options:[`was opened`,`opened`,`is opened`,`has opened`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-097`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`You ___ bring food to the party. We have plenty.`,explanation:`Use 'don't have to' when something is not necessary. Example: You don't have to come if you're tired.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'have to' means it is necessary, but they already have plenty.`,1:`'must' shows strong obligation, which does not fit 'We have plenty'.`,2:`'should' advises bringing food, but it isn't needed.`},options:[`have to`,`must`,`should`,`don't have to`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-098`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`Did there use to ___ a bakery on this corner?`,explanation:`Question form: Did there use to be...? Example: Did there use to be a lake here?`,wrongReasons:{1:`After 'use to', use the base form, not -ing.`,2:`After 'use to', use the base form, not the past participle.`,3:`After 'use to', use the base form 'be', not 'is'.`},options:[`be`,`being`,`been`,`is`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-099`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`Lucy has just ___ an email to her manager.`,explanation:`Use has/have + just + past participle for a very recent action. Example: He has just left.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'send' is the base form; after 'has just' you need the past participle.`,1:`'sending' is the -ing form and does not follow 'has'.`,2:`'sends' is the present simple form, not a participle.`},options:[`send`,`sending`,`sends`,`sent`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-100`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`Where were you going when I ___ you yesterday?`,explanation:`Use the past simple for the short action. Example: What were you doing when I called?`,wrongReasons:{0:`'see' is not usually used in the continuous form for a short moment.`,1:`The present simple does not fit with 'yesterday'.`,3:`The present perfect is not used with 'yesterday'.`},options:[`was seeing`,`see`,`saw`,`have seen`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-101`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If you don't hurry, you ___ late for school.`,explanation:`First conditional for warnings: if + present simple, will + verb. Example: If you touch that, you'll get hurt.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'would be' is for imaginary situations, not a real warning.`,1:`The past simple does not show a future result.`,3:`The present perfect does not show a future result.`},options:[`would be`,`were`,`will be`,`have been`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-102`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The tickets ___ online, not at the door.`,explanation:`Present passive: are + past participle. Example: Newspapers are sold at the station.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'tickets' is plural, so you need 'are'.`,2:`This is active; the tickets are not doing the selling.`,3:`This is not a full verb; you need 'are sold'.`},options:[`are sold`,`is sold`,`are selling`,`selling`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-103`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Children ___ play with matches. It's dangerous.`,explanation:`Use 'shouldn't' to advise against something. Example: You shouldn't eat so much sugar.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'should' advises them to do it, but it's dangerous.`,2:`'don't have to' only means it isn't necessary; it doesn't warn about danger.`,3:`'might' shows possibility, not advice or a warning.`},options:[`shouldn't`,`should`,`don't have to`,`might`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-104`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`My mum didn't ___ drive, but she got her licence last year.`,explanation:`Negative: didn't + use to + base form. Example: He didn't use to like dogs.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'didn't', write 'use to' without -d.`,2:`'using to' is not a correct form.`,3:`'uses to' is not a correct form after 'didn't'.`},options:[`used to`,`use to`,`using to`,`uses to`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-105`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`How many countries ___ visited so far?`,explanation:`Use the present perfect with 'so far' for a total up to now. Example: How many books have you read so far?`,wrongReasons:{0:`'so far' means up to now, so the past simple does not fit.`,1:`In a question, 'have' comes before the subject.`,3:`'are' does not form the present perfect.`},options:[`did you`,`you have`,`have you`,`are you`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-106`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`Kate ___ her bike when she fell off.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for the longer action and the past simple for the short one. Example: He was cooking when he burned his finger.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present continuous does not fit with the past 'fell'.`,1:`The present perfect does not describe an action in progress in the past.`,2:`The present simple talks about habits, not a past moment.`},options:[`is riding`,`has ridden`,`rides`,`was riding`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-107`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`The alarm rings if someone ___ the door.`,explanation:`Zero conditional: present simple in both clauses. Example: The light comes on if you clap.`,wrongReasons:{0:`In a zero conditional, use the present simple after 'if'.`,1:`The past simple does not fit with 'rings'.`,3:`The present continuous does not fit a general rule like this.`},options:[`will open`,`opened`,`opens`,`is opening`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-108`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`We ___ use our phones in class last year, but now it's allowed.`,explanation:`Use 'couldn't' for something that was not allowed in the past. Example: We couldn't go out after ten.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'can't' is present, but 'last year' is past.`,1:`'mustn't' has no past form and does not go with 'last year'.`,2:`'don't have to' is present and means it isn't necessary.`},options:[`can't`,`mustn't`,`don't have to`,`couldn't`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-109`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`We used to live in Spain, but we ___ there now.`,explanation:`Compare a past habit (used to) with the present simple for now. Example: I used to smoke, but I don't smoke now.`,wrongReasons:{0:`This says it wasn't a past habit, but they did live there.`,2:`A present negative needs 'don't'.`,3:`'doesn't' is for he/she/it, not 'we'.`},options:[`didn't use to live`,`don't live`,`not live`,`doesn't live`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-110`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`She enjoys ___ in the sea early in the morning.`,explanation:`'enjoy' is followed by the -ing form: I enjoy reading.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'enjoy' is followed by the -ing form, not 'to' + verb.`,2:`After 'enjoy' we cannot use the base form.`,3:`'swam' is the past simple; after 'enjoy' we need the -ing form.`},options:[`to swim`,`swimming`,`swim`,`swam`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-111`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`We decided ___ the train instead of driving.`,explanation:`'decide' is followed by 'to' + base verb: They decided to leave early.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'decide' is followed by 'to' + verb, not the -ing form.`,1:`After 'decide' we need 'to' before the verb.`,3:`'took' is a past form; after 'decide' use 'to' + base verb.`},options:[`taking`,`take`,`to take`,`took`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-112`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`My brother has given up ___ fizzy drinks.`,explanation:`'give up' (= stop doing) is followed by the -ing form: She gave up eating meat.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'give up' is followed by the -ing form, not the infinitive.`,1:`After a phrasal verb like 'give up' we cannot use the base form.`,2:`'drunk' is the past participle; 'give up' needs the -ing form.`},options:[`to drink`,`drink`,`drunk`,`drinking`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-113`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`The museum guide offered ___ us around the old castle.`,explanation:`'offer' is followed by 'to' + base verb: He offered to help me.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'offer' takes 'to' + verb, not the -ing form.`,2:`After 'offer' we must use 'to' before the verb.`,3:`'shown' is the past participle and does not follow 'offer'.`},options:[`showing`,`to show`,`show`,`shown`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-114`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Would you mind ___ the window? It's cold in here.`,explanation:`'Would you mind' is followed by the -ing form: Would you mind waiting?`,wrongReasons:{1:`'mind' is followed by the -ing form, never by 'to' + verb.`,2:`After 'Would you mind' the base form is not possible.`,3:`'closed' is a past form; polite requests with 'mind' use the -ing form.`},options:[`closing`,`to close`,`close`,`closed`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-115`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`I'm really looking forward to ___ my cousins in Canada.`,explanation:`In 'look forward to', 'to' is a preposition, so it is followed by -ing: I look forward to meeting you.`,wrongReasons:{0:`In 'look forward to', 'to' is a preposition, so the base form cannot follow it.`,1:`'to' is already in the phrase; 'to to see' is wrong, and the verb must be -ing.`,3:`'saw' is the past simple and cannot follow the preposition 'to'.`},options:[`see`,`to see`,`seeing`,`saw`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-116`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`The scientists hope ___ the results next spring.`,explanation:`'hope' is followed by 'to' + base verb: We hope to see you soon.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'hope' is followed by 'to' + verb, not the -ing form.`,2:`After 'hope' we need 'to' before the base verb.`,3:`'published' is a past form and cannot follow 'hope' here.`},options:[`publishing`,`to publish`,`publish`,`published`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-117`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`He's very good at ___ old bicycles.`,explanation:`After a preposition (at, in, of, about) use the -ing form: She is good at drawing.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After a preposition like 'at' we cannot use 'to' + verb.`,2:`A preposition is never followed by the base form.`,3:`'repaired' is a past form; after 'at' we need the -ing form.`},options:[`to repair`,`repairing`,`repair`,`repaired`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-118`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Please remember ___ the lights off when you leave the office.`,explanation:`'remember to do' means not forget a future action: Remember to lock the door.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'remember + -ing' is for memories of the past, not for something you must do later.`,2:`After 'remember' the base form without 'to' is not correct.`,3:`'turned' is the past simple and does not fit after 'remember'.`},options:[`to turn`,`turning`,`turn`,`turned`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-119`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`The lawyer suggested ___ the contract again before signing it.`,explanation:`'suggest' is followed by the -ing form: He suggested going by bus.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'suggest' is not followed by 'to' + verb.`,1:`'suggest' is not followed by the base form alone.`,3:`'to reading' is not a correct pattern after 'suggest'.`},options:[`to read`,`read`,`reading`,`to reading`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-120`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Our neighbours can't afford ___ a new car this year.`,explanation:`'afford' is followed by 'to' + base verb: I can't afford to travel this summer.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'afford' takes 'to' + verb, not the -ing form.`,2:`After 'afford' we need 'to' before the verb.`,3:`'bought' is a past form; 'afford' needs 'to' + base verb.`},options:[`to buy`,`buying`,`buy`,`bought`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-121`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`I avoid ___ the motorway on Friday evenings because of the traffic.`,explanation:`'avoid' is followed by the -ing form: Try to avoid eating late at night.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'avoid' is followed by the -ing form, not 'to' + verb.`,1:`The base form cannot follow 'avoid'.`,2:`'used' is the past simple; after 'avoid' we need the -ing form.`},options:[`to use`,`use`,`used`,`using`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-122`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Tom promised ___ late again.`,explanation:`'promise' takes 'to' + verb; the negative is 'not to' + verb: She promised not to tell anyone.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'promise' is followed by 'to' + verb, so the negative is 'not to be', not 'not being'.`,1:`'don't be' is an imperative and cannot follow 'promise'.`,3:`The infinitive after 'promise' needs 'to': 'not to be'.`},options:[`not being`,`don't be`,`not to be`,`not be`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-123`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ vegetables in the garden is my grandmother's favourite hobby.`,explanation:`The -ing form can be the subject of a sentence: Swimming is good for you.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The base form cannot be the subject of a sentence.`,2:`'Grew' is the past simple and cannot be the subject.`,3:`'Grows' is a verb with -s, not a noun; the subject needs the -ing form.`},options:[`Grow`,`Growing`,`Grew`,`Grows`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-124`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`The children kept ___ questions during the film.`,explanation:`'keep' (= continue doing) is followed by the -ing form: He kept talking.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'keep' (= continue) is followed by the -ing form, not 'to' + verb.`,1:`After 'keep' we cannot use the base form.`,2:`'asked' is a past form; 'keep' needs the -ing form.`},options:[`to ask`,`ask`,`asked`,`asking`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-125`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Visitors are not allowed ___ photos inside the gallery.`,explanation:`'be allowed' is followed by 'to' + base verb: You are not allowed to park here.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'be allowed' is followed by 'to' + verb, not the -ing form.`,1:`After 'be allowed' we need 'to' before the verb.`,3:`'took' is a past form and cannot follow 'allowed'.`},options:[`taking`,`take`,`to take`,`took`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-126`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Thank you for ___ me with my homework.`,explanation:`'Thank you for' is followed by the -ing form because 'for' is a preposition: Thank you for coming.`,wrongReasons:{1:`After the preposition 'for' we cannot use 'to' + verb.`,2:`A preposition like 'for' is not followed by the base form.`,3:`'helped' is a past form; after 'for' we need the -ing form.`},options:[`helping`,`to help`,`help`,`helped`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-127`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`The engineers managed ___ the bridge before winter.`,explanation:`'manage' (= succeed in doing something difficult) is followed by 'to' + verb: She managed to open the jar.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'manage' takes 'to' + verb, not the -ing form.`,2:`After 'manage' we need 'to' before the base verb.`,3:`'finished' is a past form; 'manage' needs 'to' + verb.`},options:[`to finish`,`finishing`,`finish`,`finished`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-129`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I've got the tickets! We ___ the band in concert on Saturday night.`,explanation:`Use the present continuous for fixed future arrangements: I'm meeting Anna on Friday.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple is not used for personal arrangements in the future.`,1:`'saw' is past; Saturday night is in the future.`,3:`The present perfect talks about the past up to now, not a future plan.`},options:[`see`,`saw`,`are seeing`,`have seen`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-130`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`A: The phone is ringing. B: I ___ it.`,explanation:`Use 'will' for decisions made at the moment of speaking: I'm thirsty. I'll get some water.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The present simple is not used for a decision made at the moment of speaking.`,2:`'answered' is past, but B has not answered yet.`,3:`'have answered' says the action is already done, which is not true.`},options:[`'ll answer`,`answer`,`answered`,`have answered`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-131`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`According to the timetable, the train to Manchester ___ at 6.15 tomorrow morning.`,explanation:`Use the present simple for timetables and schedules: The film starts at eight.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'leave' without -s does not agree with 'the train'.`,2:`'left' is past, but the time is tomorrow morning.`,3:`'has left' is the present perfect and cannot be used with 'tomorrow'.`},options:[`leaves`,`leave`,`left`,`has left`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-133`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`What ___ this weekend? Have you got any plans?`,explanation:`Use the present continuous to ask about future plans and arrangements: What are you doing tonight?`,wrongReasons:{0:`'do you do' asks about habits or jobs, not plans for this weekend.`,1:`'did you do' asks about the past, not the coming weekend.`,3:`'have you done' asks about past experience, not future plans.`},options:[`do you do`,`did you do`,`are you doing`,`have you done`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-134`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`She has saved enough money, and she ___ a new laptop next month.`,explanation:`Use 'be going to' for plans and intentions: I'm going to learn Spanish next year.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple is not used for personal future intentions.`,1:`'bought' is past, but the time is next month.`,2:`'has bought' is the present perfect and does not match 'next month'.`},options:[`buys`,`bought`,`has bought`,`is going to buy`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-135`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I think robots ___ many jobs in factories in the future.`,explanation:`Use 'will' for predictions based on opinion, often after 'I think': I think it will be hot tomorrow.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The present simple does not express a prediction after 'I think ... in the future'.`,2:`'did' is past, but the sentence is about the future.`,3:`'have done' looks at the past, not the future.`},options:[`will do`,`do`,`did`,`have done`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-136`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`Be careful! You ___ that glass of water!`,explanation:`Use 'be going to' when we can see that something is about to happen: Watch out! You're going to fall.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple does not warn about something that is about to happen.`,2:`'dropped' is past, but the glass has not fallen yet.`,3:`'have dropped' means it has already happened, so 'Be careful!' makes no sense.`},options:[`drop`,`'re going to drop`,`dropped`,`have dropped`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-137`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`Don't worry about the heavy bags. I ___ you carry them.`,explanation:`Use 'will' to offer to do something: That looks heavy. I'll carry it for you.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple is not used to make an offer.`,1:`'helped' is past, but the offer is for now or later.`,2:`'have helped' says it is finished, but no help has happened yet.`},options:[`help`,`helped`,`have helped`,`'ll help`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-138`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`Our flight ___ at 10 a.m., so we need to be at the airport by eight.`,explanation:`Use the present simple for official timetables: The plane lands at 9.30.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'take off' needs -s with 'our flight' (he/she/it).`,2:`'took off' is past, but they still need to go to the airport.`,3:`'has taken off' says the plane has already left.`},options:[`takes off`,`take off`,`took off`,`has taken off`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-139`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`My sister ___ married in June. She has already booked the hall.`,explanation:`Use the present continuous for arrangements that are already planned: We're moving house next week.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The present simple is used for timetables, not for personal arrangements like a wedding.`,2:`'got' is past, but June is in the future here.`,3:`'has got married' would mean it already happened; 'has got' does not fit.`},options:[`is getting`,`gets`,`got`,`has got`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-140`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I'm sure you ___ the exam. You've studied so hard.`,explanation:`Use 'will' for predictions, especially after 'I'm sure' or 'I think': I'm sure she'll love the present.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple does not express a prediction after 'I'm sure'.`,2:`'passed' is past, but the exam has not happened yet.`,3:`The present continuous is for arrangements, not predictions like this one.`},options:[`pass`,`'ll pass`,`passed`,`are passing`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-141`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`Wait for me here. I ___ back in five minutes.`,explanation:`Use 'will' for promises and quick statements about the near future: I'll be ready soon.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'being' is not a complete verb and cannot follow 'I' alone.`,2:`'was' is past, but 'in five minutes' is the future.`,3:`'have been' is the present perfect and does not match 'in five minutes'.`},options:[`'ll be`,`being`,`was`,`have been`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-142`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`It was announced today: the government ___ a new law about plastic bags next year.`,explanation:`Use 'be going to' for decided plans: The city is going to build a new bridge.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple is not used for a plan like this; it is for timetables and habits.`,1:`'introduced' is past, but the time is next year.`,3:`'has introduced' talks about the past, not next year.`},options:[`introduces`,`introduced`,`is going to introduce`,`has introduced`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-143`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`A: We've run out of milk. B: Oh, really? I ___ some on my way home.`,explanation:`Use 'will' for a decision made at the moment of speaking: It's cold. I'll close the window.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple is not used for a decision you make while speaking.`,1:`'bought' is past; B has just learned about the milk.`,3:`'have bought' says it is already done, which is not true.`},options:[`buy`,`bought`,`'ll buy`,`have bought`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-144`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`___ I carry your suitcase for you? It looks heavy.`,explanation:`Use 'Shall I...?' to offer help: Shall I open the door for you?`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Do I carry...?' asks about a habit, not an offer.`,1:`'Am I carry' is wrong; 'am' cannot go with the base form 'carry'.`,3:`'Have I carry' is wrong; 'have' needs a past participle, and it is not an offer.`},options:[`Do`,`Am`,`Shall`,`Have`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-145`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`Next year my parents ___ their thirtieth wedding anniversary.`,explanation:`Use 'will' (or 'be going to') to talk about future events: Next month I will be twenty.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'celebrated' is past, but the time is next year.`,1:`'have celebrated' is about the past, not next year.`,3:`'celebrating' needs 'are' before it to make a verb.`},options:[`celebrated`,`have celebrated`,`will celebrate`,`celebrating`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-148`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`I lost the umbrella ___ my aunt gave me.`,explanation:`Use 'that' or 'which' for things: The book that you lent me is great.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'where' is for places, not objects like an umbrella.`,1:`'who' is for people, not things.`,2:`'what' cannot follow a noun to start a relative clause.`},options:[`where`,`who`,`what`,`that`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-149`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The scientist ___ discovered this medicine won an important prize.`,explanation:`Use 'who' for people: The girl who sings in the choir is my sister.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'which' is used for things, but a scientist is a person.`,2:`'where' refers to a place.`,3:`'what' does not follow a noun in a relative clause.`},options:[`who`,`which`,`where`,`what`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-150`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Do you know the name of the town ___ the famous painter was born?`,explanation:`Use 'where' after a noun for a place: This is the village where I grew up.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'which' would need 'in' after 'born' ('which he was born in').`,1:`'who' is for people, but the gap refers to the town.`,2:`'when' is for times, not places.`},options:[`which`,`who`,`when`,`where`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-151`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The laptop ___ I bought last week has already stopped working.`,explanation:`Use 'which' or 'that' for things: The car which he drives is very old.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'who' is for people, not objects.`,2:`'where' is for places; a laptop is a thing.`,3:`'whose' shows possession and needs a noun after it.`},options:[`who`,`which`,`where`,`whose`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-152`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Is that the boy ___ dog bit the postman?`,explanation:`Use 'whose' to show possession: That's the woman whose car was stolen.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'who' cannot show possession; we need 'whose dog'.`,2:`'who's' means 'who is', which does not fit before 'dog'.`,3:`'which' is for things and does not show possession here.`},options:[`whose`,`who`,`who's`,`which`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-153`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The film ___ we watched last night was really boring.`,explanation:`Use 'that' or 'which' for things: The song that she sang was beautiful.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'who' is only for people.`,2:`'where' is for places, not films.`,3:`'what' cannot follow a noun like 'the film'.`},options:[`that`,`who`,`where`,`what`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-154`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`A dictionary is a book ___ tells you the meaning of words.`,explanation:`Use 'which' or 'that' for things: A fridge is a machine which keeps food cold.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'who' is for people, but a book is a thing.`,1:`'where' is for places.`,2:`'what' is not used after a noun to describe it.`},options:[`who`,`where`,`what`,`which`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-155`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The police officer ___ helped us was very kind.`,explanation:`Use 'who' or 'that' for people in defining relative clauses: The teacher that helped me was great.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'which' is for things, not people.`,1:`'where' is for places, not people.`,2:`'whose' must be followed by a noun, like 'whose car'.`},options:[`which`,`where`,`whose`,`that`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-156`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`This is the café ___ my parents first met.`,explanation:`Use 'where' for a place: That's the park where we play football.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'which' would need 'in' or 'at' ('which my parents first met in').`,2:`'who' refers to people, not a café.`,3:`'whose' shows possession and needs a noun after it.`},options:[`where`,`which`,`who`,`whose`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-157`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`My grandfather, ___ is 85, still goes swimming every day.`,explanation:`In a clause between commas, use 'who' for people and 'which' for things, never 'that': My aunt, who lives in Paris, is a doctor.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'that' cannot be used in a clause between commas.`,1:`'which' is for things, not people.`,3:`'whose' must be followed by a noun.`},options:[`that`,`which`,`who`,`whose`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-158`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Paris, ___ I visited in 2019, is full of beautiful buildings.`,explanation:`In a clause between commas, use 'which' for things and places when the verb needs an object: Rome, which I love, is very old.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'that' is not used in a clause between commas.`,1:`'where' is wrong because 'visit' needs an object: we visit a place, not visit 'in' it.`,2:`'who' is for people, not cities.`},options:[`that`,`where`,`who`,`which`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-159`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`I have a friend ___ brother plays in a rock band.`,explanation:`Use 'whose' + noun to show possession: I know a girl whose mother is a pilot.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'who brother' is wrong; to show possession we need 'whose'.`,2:`'that' cannot show possession.`,3:`'which' is for things and does not show possession.`},options:[`who`,`whose`,`that`,`which`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-160`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The students ___ passed the test will get a certificate.`,explanation:`Use 'who' or 'that' for people: The players who won got a medal.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'which' is for things, not students.`,2:`'whose' needs a noun after it, like 'whose names'.`,3:`'where' refers to a place, not people.`},options:[`who`,`which`,`whose`,`where`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-161`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The park ___ we used to play as children is now a car park.`,explanation:`Use 'where' for places when there is no preposition at the end: This is the room where I sleep.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'which' would need 'in' at the end ('which we used to play in').`,1:`'who' is for people, not a park.`,2:`'that' would need 'in' at the end ('that we used to play in').`},options:[`which`,`who`,`that`,`where`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-162`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`What's the name of the app ___ helps you learn languages?`,explanation:`Use 'that' or 'which' for things: I have an app that counts my steps.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'who' is for people, not apps.`,1:`'where' is for places.`,3:`'whose' shows possession and must have a noun after it.`},options:[`who`,`where`,`that`,`whose`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-163`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Can you ___ my cat while I'm on holiday?`,explanation:`'look after' means take care of someone or something: She looks after her little brother.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'look for' means try to find something.`,2:`'look up' means find information, for example in a dictionary.`,3:`'look into' means investigate a problem.`},options:[`look for`,`look after`,`look up`,`look into`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-164`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`I don't know this word. I'll look it ___ in the dictionary.`,explanation:`'look something up' means find information in a book or online: Look it up on the internet.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'look after' means take care of someone.`,1:`'look for' means try to find something you have lost.`,3:`'look out' means be careful.`},options:[`after`,`for`,`up`,`out`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-165`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`It's hot in here. You can ___ your jacket if you like.`,explanation:`'take off' means remove clothes: Take off your shoes, please.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'put on' means start wearing, which is the opposite of what you want when it's hot.`,1:`'take up' means start a new hobby.`,3:`'turn off' is for machines and lights, not clothes.`},options:[`put on`,`take up`,`take off`,`turn off`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-166`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The plane ___ late because of the fog.`,explanation:`'take off' (for planes) means leave the ground: The plane took off at noon.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'took up' means started a hobby.`,1:`'took after' means looked or behaved like a parent.`,2:`'took over' means took control of something.`},options:[`took up`,`took after`,`took over`,`took off`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-167`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`She ___ her mother: they both have curly red hair.`,explanation:`'take after' means look or behave like an older family member: He takes after his father.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'takes off' means removes or leaves the ground.`,2:`'looks after' means takes care of someone.`,3:`'brings up' means raises a child.`},options:[`takes after`,`takes off`,`looks after`,`brings up`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-168`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`We've ___ sugar, so I can't make a cake.`,explanation:`'run out of' means have no more of something: We've run out of bread.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'run into' means meet someone by chance.`,1:`'run away from' means escape from a place or person.`,3:`'run over' means hit someone or something with a car.`},options:[`run into`,`run away from`,`run out of`,`run over`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-169`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Can you ___ the music? I'm trying to sleep.`,explanation:`'turn down' means make a sound quieter: Please turn down the TV.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'turn up' means make it louder, the opposite of what you need.`,2:`'turn on' means start a machine.`,3:`'turn into' means change into something different.`},options:[`turn up`,`turn down`,`turn on`,`turn into`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-170`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The meeting was ___ because the manager was ill.`,explanation:`'call off' means cancel: They called off the match because of the snow.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'called back' means phoned someone again.`,2:`'put on' means organised an event, which does not match an ill manager.`,3:`'taken off' is for clothes or planes, not meetings.`},options:[`called back`,`called off`,`put on`,`taken off`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-171`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`I ___ an old school friend at the station yesterday. What a surprise!`,explanation:`'run into' means meet someone by chance: I ran into my teacher at the market.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'ran out' means had no more of something.`,1:`'ran after' means chased someone.`,3:`'ran over' means hit someone with a car.`},options:[`ran out`,`ran after`,`ran into`,`ran over`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-172`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`My grandparents ___ me up because my parents worked abroad.`,explanation:`'bring up' means look after a child until it is an adult: She was brought up by her aunt.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'grow up' cannot have an object: you grow up, but nobody 'grows you up'.`,1:`'look up' means find information, not raise a child.`,3:`'take up' means start a hobby.`},options:[`grew`,`looked`,`brought`,`took`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-173`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`I'm going to ___ smoking next month. It's bad for my health.`,explanation:`'give up' means stop doing something: He gave up coffee last year.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'give in' means stop fighting or arguing and accept something.`,2:`'give back' means return something to its owner.`,3:`'give away' means give something free to someone.`},options:[`give up`,`give in`,`give back`,`give away`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-174`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`The firefighters quickly ___ the fire.`,explanation:`'put out' means stop a fire burning: Put out the candle before you go to bed.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'put off' means delay something until later.`,1:`'put on' means start wearing or switch on.`,2:`'put up' means build or fix something on a wall.`},options:[`put off`,`put on`,`put up`,`put out`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-175`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`We had to ___ the football match until next week because of the rain.`,explanation:`'put off' means move something to a later time: Don't put off your homework.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'put out' means stop a fire.`,2:`'put up' means build or hang something.`,3:`'put down' means place something on a surface.`},options:[`put off`,`put out`,`put up`,`put down`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-176`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`I usually ___ at 6.30 on weekdays, but I stay in bed later at the weekend.`,explanation:`'get up' means leave your bed in the morning: I get up early every day.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'get on' means enter a bus or train, or have a good relationship.`,1:`'get off' means leave a bus or train.`,3:`'get over' means recover from an illness or a problem.`},options:[`get on`,`get off`,`get up`,`get over`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-177`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`It took him weeks to ___ his cold.`,explanation:`'get over' means recover from an illness or a bad experience: She got over the flu quickly.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'get up' means leave your bed.`,1:`'get off' means leave a bus or train.`,2:`'get on' means enter a bus or have a good relationship.`},options:[`get up`,`get off`,`get on`,`get over`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-178`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Can you ___ the children from school at 3 o'clock? I have a meeting.`,explanation:`'pick up' means collect someone, often by car: I'll pick you up at the station.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'pick out' means choose one thing from a group.`,1:`'take up' means start a hobby.`,2:`'pick on' means treat someone badly.`},options:[`pick out`,`take up`,`pick on`,`pick up`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-179`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Our car ___ on the way to the airport, so we took a taxi.`,explanation:`'break down' means stop working (for machines and cars): The bus broke down in the rain.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'broke up' means ended a relationship.`,1:`'broke out' means started suddenly, like a war or a fire.`,3:`'broke in' means entered a building illegally.`},options:[`broke up`,`broke out`,`broke down`,`broke in`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-180`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`___! There's a car coming!`,explanation:`'Look out!' means be careful because of danger: Look out! The floor is wet.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Look up' means search for information or raise your eyes.`,1:`'Look after' means take care of someone.`,2:`'Look into' means investigate.`},options:[`Look up`,`Look after`,`Look into`,`Look out`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-181`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`I stayed at home ___ I had a bad headache.`,explanation:`'because' introduces a reason: I was late because I missed the bus.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'so' introduces a result, but the headache is the reason.`,1:`'although' shows a contrast, but there is no contrast here.`,3:`'however' is used at the start of a new sentence, not to join two clauses like this.`},options:[`so`,`although`,`because`,`however`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-182`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`It was raining, ___ we took our umbrellas.`,explanation:`'so' introduces a result: I was hungry, so I made a sandwich.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'because' introduces a reason, but taking umbrellas is a result.`,2:`'although' shows contrast, but this is cause and result.`,3:`'unless' means 'if not', which makes no sense here.`},options:[`so`,`because`,`although`,`unless`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-183`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ he trained hard, he didn't win the race.`,explanation:`'although' shows contrast between two ideas: Although it was late, we kept working.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Because' gives a reason, but training hard is not a reason for losing.`,2:`'So' introduces a result and cannot start this kind of clause.`,3:`'However' cannot join two clauses in one sentence like this.`},options:[`Although`,`Because`,`So`,`However`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-184`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`The hotel was expensive. ___, the staff were very friendly.`,explanation:`'However' introduces a contrast at the start of a new sentence, followed by a comma: It was cold. However, we went out.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Because' cannot start a sentence followed by a comma like this; it needs a clause.`,2:`'So' shows a result, but friendly staff is not a result of high prices.`,3:`'Although' must join two clauses in the same sentence.`},options:[`Because`,`However`,`So`,`Although`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-185`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`I'd like to come to the party, ___ I have to work that evening.`,explanation:`'but' joins two contrasting ideas: I like tennis, but I don't play it often.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'so' shows a result, but working is not a result of wanting to come.`,1:`'because' gives a reason, but working is not why you want to come.`,2:`'or' gives a choice, which does not fit here.`},options:[`so`,`because`,`or`,`but`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-186`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`She went to bed early ___ she was very tired.`,explanation:`'because' gives the reason for something: He smiled because he was happy.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'although' shows contrast, but being tired is a reason to go to bed.`,2:`'so' introduces a result, but being tired is the cause here.`,3:`'but' shows contrast, which does not fit this cause.`},options:[`although`,`because`,`so`,`but`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-187`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`We missed the bus, ___ we were late for the lesson.`,explanation:`'so' introduces the result of something: It was sunny, so we went to the beach.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'because' gives a reason, but being late is the result here.`,1:`'although' shows contrast, but this is cause and result.`,2:`'unless' means 'if not', which does not fit.`},options:[`because`,`although`,`unless`,`so`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-188`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ the weather was bad, the festival continued.`,explanation:`'Although' + clause shows contrast: Although she was ill, she went to work.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Because of' is followed by a noun, and it gives a reason, not a contrast.`,2:`'Despite' is followed by a noun or -ing, not a full clause like 'the weather was bad'.`,3:`'However' cannot join two clauses in one sentence like this.`},options:[`Because of`,`Although`,`Despite`,`However`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-189`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`My phone wasn't working. ___, I couldn't call you.`,explanation:`'As a result' introduces a result at the start of a new sentence: The road was closed. As a result, we were late.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Although' shows contrast and must join two clauses in one sentence.`,2:`'Because' gives a reason and cannot be followed by a comma like this.`,3:`'Unless' means 'if not' and does not fit a result.`},options:[`Although`,`As a result`,`Because`,`Unless`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-190`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`You can't drive a car ___ you have a licence.`,explanation:`'unless' means 'if not': You can't enter unless you have a ticket.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'if' makes the sentence mean you can't drive when you have a licence, which is wrong.`,2:`'because' gives a reason, which makes the meaning strange.`,3:`'so' introduces a result, which does not fit here.`},options:[`if`,`unless`,`because`,`so`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-191`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`I like coffee, ___ my wife prefers tea.`,explanation:`'but' shows a contrast between two ideas: He is tall, but his brother is short.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'so' shows a result, but my wife's taste is not a result of mine.`,1:`'because' gives a reason, which does not fit here.`,2:`'unless' means 'if not', which makes no sense here.`},options:[`so`,`because`,`unless`,`but`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-192`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`We stayed inside ___ the storm.`,explanation:`'because of' + noun gives a reason: The match was cancelled because of the rain.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'because' must be followed by a clause with a verb, not just a noun.`,2:`'although' needs a clause and shows contrast, not a reason.`,3:`'so' introduces a result and needs a clause.`},options:[`because of`,`because`,`although`,`so`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-193`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ I was walking home, I saw an accident.`,explanation:`'while' + clause means 'at the same time as': While I was cooking, the phone rang.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'During' is followed by a noun, not a clause with a verb.`,2:`'Because of' is followed by a noun and gives a reason.`,3:`'Despite' shows contrast and is followed by a noun or -ing.`},options:[`During`,`While`,`Because of`,`Despite`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-194`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`We went swimming in the sea ___ the water was very cold.`,explanation:`'even though' is a strong way to show contrast: She went out even though it was raining.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'because' gives a reason, but cold water is not a reason to swim.`,2:`'so that' shows a purpose, which does not fit here.`,3:`'unless' means 'if not', which makes no sense here.`},options:[`even though`,`because`,`so that`,`unless`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-195`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Turn off the lights when you leave ___ save energy.`,explanation:`Use 'to' + base verb to show purpose: I went to the shop to buy milk.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'for' is followed by a noun or -ing, not the base verb.`,2:`'so' needs a clause with a subject, like 'so we save energy'.`,3:`'because' needs a full clause with a subject.`},options:[`for`,`to`,`so`,`because`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-196`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`I've never been to Japan. ___, I'd love to go one day.`,explanation:`'However' shows contrast with the sentence before: I'm tired. However, I'll finish the work.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Because' gives a reason and cannot be followed by a comma at the start like this.`,1:`'So that' shows purpose and needs to join two clauses.`,2:`'Unless' means 'if not', which does not fit here.`},options:[`Because`,`So that`,`Unless`,`However`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-197`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`First, mix the flour and the eggs. Then add the milk. ___, put the mixture in the oven.`,explanation:`'Finally' introduces the last step in a list of instructions: Finally, serve the soup hot.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Although' shows contrast and needs two clauses.`,2:`'Because' gives a reason, not the last step.`,3:`'However' shows contrast, not the order of steps.`},options:[`Finally`,`Although`,`Because`,`However`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-198`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Could you ___ me your pen for a minute?`,explanation:`'lend' means give something to someone for a short time: Can you lend me your bike?`,wrongReasons:{0:`You 'borrow' something from someone; you don't 'borrow me' something.`,1:`'rent' is for paying money to use something, like a flat.`,2:`'take me your pen' is wrong; 'take' does not mean give.`},options:[`borrow`,`rent`,`take`,`lend`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-199`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`I need to ___ some money from the bank to buy a house.`,explanation:`'borrow' means take something that you will give back later: I borrowed a book from the library.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'lend' is what the bank does; you take money, so you borrow.`,2:`'spend' means use money to buy things, not get it from a bank.`,3:`'pay' means give money to someone, not receive it.`},options:[`borrow`,`lend`,`spend`,`pay`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-201`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`We ___ the bus by two minutes and had to wait half an hour for the next one.`,explanation:`'miss' a bus, train or plane means arrive too late to catch it: Hurry or we'll miss the train.`,wrongReasons:{0:`You 'lose' things like keys; for transport you didn't catch, use 'miss'.`,1:`'fail' is for exams or tests, not buses.`,2:`'passed' would mean the bus went by you, not that you arrived too late.`},options:[`lost`,`failed`,`passed`,`missed`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-202`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`She works very hard and ___ a good salary.`,explanation:`'earn' means get money for work: He earns 2,000 euros a month.`,wrongReasons:{0:`You 'win' a prize or a game, not a salary.`,1:`'gain' is used for weight or experience, not a salary.`,2:`'reach' means arrive at a place or level, not get money for work.`},options:[`wins`,`gains`,`reaches`,`earns`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-203`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Can you ___ me to buy some bread on the way home?`,explanation:`'remind someone to do something' means help them not forget: Remind me to call Mum.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'remember' is not followed by 'me to'; you remember something yourself.`,2:`'recall' means remember something from the past.`,3:`'forget' is the opposite of what you want.`},options:[`remember`,`remind`,`recall`,`forget`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-204`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The ___ for the concert were sold out in an hour.`,explanation:`A 'ticket' lets you enter an event or travel: I bought two tickets for the match.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'receipts' are papers you get after paying.`,2:`'notes' are short written messages or paper money.`,3:`'bills' tell you how much you need to pay.`},options:[`receipts`,`tickets`,`notes`,`bills`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-205`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Our new flat is very ___: the shops and the station are only five minutes away.`,explanation:`'convenient' means easy and useful, often because it is near: The hotel is convenient for the airport.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'comfortable' describes how nice it feels inside, not the distance to shops.`,1:`'careful' describes people who avoid danger, not places.`,3:`'lonely' describes a sad feeling of being alone.`},options:[`comfortable`,`careful`,`convenient`,`lonely`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-206`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`I'm ___ in history, so I often watch documentaries about ancient Rome.`,explanation:`Use '-ed' adjectives for how people feel: I'm interested in music. The film was interesting.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'interesting' describes the thing, not how a person feels.`,2:`'bored' means you don't like it, which doesn't match watching documentaries.`,3:`'boring' describes a thing and has the opposite meaning.`},options:[`interesting`,`interested`,`bored`,`boring`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-207`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Please ___ the dishes after dinner.`,explanation:`We say 'do the dishes' (= wash them): I'll do the dishes tonight.`,wrongReasons:{0:`We 'make' a meal or a cake, but we 'do' the dishes.`,1:`'take the dishes' means carry them, not wash them.`,3:`'have the dishes' does not mean wash them.`},options:[`make`,`take`,`do`,`have`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-208`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`I'm going to ___ a cake for my brother's birthday.`,explanation:`We 'make' food such as cakes and sandwiches: She made a pizza for dinner.`,wrongReasons:{0:`We 'do' jobs and homework, but we 'make' food like cakes.`,2:`'cook a cake' is not natural; we say 'make' or 'bake' a cake.`,3:`'take a cake' means carry it, not prepare it.`},options:[`do`,`make`,`cook`,`take`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-209`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`The weather ___ says it will be sunny tomorrow.`,explanation:`A 'weather forecast' says what the weather will be like: Check the forecast before you go.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'prediction' is general; for weather we say 'forecast'.`,2:`A 'guess' is an answer without information, not a weather report.`,3:`A 'view' is what you can see or an opinion.`},options:[`forecast`,`prediction`,`guess`,`view`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-210`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`He ___ his keys in the park and never found them again.`,explanation:`'lose' means not be able to find something: I lost my wallet yesterday.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'missed' is for buses, trains or people you didn't see, not keys.`,1:`'fail' is for exams and tests.`,2:`'waste' means use badly, like time or money.`},options:[`missed`,`failed`,`wasted`,`lost`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-211`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Excuse me, how much does this jacket ___?`,explanation:`Use 'cost' to talk about the price of something: How much does it cost?`,wrongReasons:{0:`People 'pay' for things; a jacket does not pay.`,1:`People 'spend' money; a jacket does not spend.`,2:`'price' is a noun here, not a verb.`},options:[`pay`,`spend`,`price`,`cost`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-212`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`I'd like to ___ a table for four people at 8 o'clock, please.`,explanation:`'book' means arrange to have something in the future: I booked a room for two nights.`,wrongReasons:{0:`You 'order' food, not a table.`,1:`'keep a table' means not give it away; it is not used for restaurants.`,3:`'rent' means pay to use something for a long time, like a flat.`},options:[`order`,`keep`,`book`,`rent`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-213`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`The ___ at the museum explained the history of every painting.`,explanation:`A 'guide' shows visitors a place and gives information: Our guide was very funny.`,wrongReasons:{0:`A 'driver' drives a car or bus.`,1:`A 'pilot' flies planes.`,2:`A 'waiter' serves food in a restaurant.`},options:[`driver`,`pilot`,`waiter`,`guide`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-214`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`This soup has no ___. Can you pass me the salt?`,explanation:`'taste' is the flavour of food: This cake has a lovely taste.`,wrongReasons:{0:`A 'view' is what you see from a window; salt doesn't change it.`,1:`'noise' is a sound, which soup doesn't have.`,3:`Salt doesn't change the 'colour' of soup.`},options:[`view`,`noise`,`taste`,`colour`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-215`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`It's a long ___ from London to Edinburgh by train.`,explanation:`A 'journey' is one trip from one place to another: The journey takes four hours.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'travel' is uncountable, so we can't say 'a long travel'.`,2:`A 'flight' is by plane, not by train.`,3:`A 'voyage' is a long trip by sea.`},options:[`travel`,`journey`,`flight`,`voyage`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-216`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The prisoners ___ after the war.`,explanation:`"Were released" is the past passive.`,options:[`have released`,`released`,`were released`,`are released`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`The prisoners did not release anyone.`,3:`"After the war" is past.`,0:`"have released" does not work here, because the prisoners did not release anyone.`}},{id:`b1-mc-217`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Which word describes a very old building where kings once lived?`,explanation:`A castle is a fortified building.`,options:[`cathedral`,`castle`,`cottage`,`cabin`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`A cottage is a small house.`,3:`A cabin is a small wooden house.`,0:`A cathedral is a large church.`}},{id:`b1-mc-218`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`___ Alps are very popular with skiers.`,explanation:`Mountain ranges take "the".`,options:[`The`,`An`,`–`,`A`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`Mountain ranges are plural names and take "the".`,2:`Names of mountain ranges take "the".`,1:`"An" cannot be used with a plural name.`}},{id:`b1-mc-219`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`___ people in this village are very friendly.`,explanation:`"The" refers to the specific people of this village.`,options:[`A`,`Some`,`The`,`An`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"A" cannot be used with the plural noun "people".`,3:`"An" cannot be used with a plural noun.`,1:`"Some" would not refer to all the villagers.`}},{id:`b1-mc-220`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Who are you waiting ___ ?`,explanation:`"Wait for" is followed by the person you expect.`,options:[`on`,`for`,`at`,`to`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Wait to" is followed by a verb.`,2:`"Wait at" is followed by a place.`,0:`"Wait on" means to serve.`}},{id:`b1-mc-221`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`If you want to lose weight, you should cut ___ on sugar.`,explanation:`"Cut down on" means to reduce.`,options:[`off`,`down`,`up`,`in`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Cut up" means to divide into pieces.`,0:`"Cut off" means to remove or isolate.`,3:`"Cut in" means to interrupt.`}},{id:`b1-mc-222`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`She ___ that the train had already left.`,explanation:`"Said that" introduces a statement.`,options:[`told`,`asked`,`said`,`spoke`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Told" needs a person after it.`,1:`"Asked" introduces a question.`,3:`"Speak" does not take "that".`}},{id:`b1-mc-223`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Which word describes the room where you cook?`,explanation:`The kitchen is where you cook.`,options:[`kitchen`,`cabinet`,`corridor`,`cellar`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`A cabinet is a piece of furniture.`,3:`A cellar is below ground.`,2:`A corridor is a passage.`}},{id:`b1-mc-224`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`He ___ be at home. His car is outside.`,explanation:`"Must be" expresses a strong deduction.`,options:[`needn't`,`shouldn't`,`can't`,`must`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Can't be" would contradict the evidence.`,0:`"Needn't" is about necessity.`,1:`"Shouldn't" gives advice.`}},{id:`b1-mc-225`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`He's a very ___ student: he always does more than is asked.`,explanation:`"Conscientious" means careful and hard-working.`,options:[`confident`,`considerate`,`conscious`,`conscientious`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Conscious" means aware.`,1:`"Considerate" means kind to others.`,0:`"Confident" means self-assured.`}},{id:`b1-mc-226`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`I'm afraid I can't ___ the noise from the neighbours any longer.`,explanation:`"Put up with" means to tolerate.`,options:[`put up with`,`put off`,`put away`,`put out`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Put off" means to delay something.`,3:`"Put out" means to extinguish a fire.`,2:`"Put away" means to store something.`}},{id:`b1-mc-227`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`The company is ___ for a new manager to join the team.`,explanation:`"Looking for" means searching for.`,options:[`finding`,`searching`,`seeking up`,`looking`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Seeking up" is not a correct phrase.`,1:`"Searching" needs "for" immediately and sounds formal.`,0:`"Finding for" is not a correct phrase.`}},{id:`b1-mc-228`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"Since" with a point in time uses the present perfect.`,options:[`I haven't seen her since Tuesday.`,`I haven't seen her for Tuesday.`,`I didn't see her since Tuesday.`,`I don't see her since Tuesday.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Since" needs the present perfect.`,1:`"For" is followed by a length of time.`,3:`"I don't see her since Tuesday." does not work here, because "Since" needs the present perfect.`}},{id:`b1-mc-229`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Of all my teachers, Mr Collins is the ___ patient.`,explanation:`Long adjectives form the superlative with "most".`,options:[`much`,`most`,`very`,`more`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"More" compares only two.`,0:`"Much" cannot form a superlative.`,2:`"Very" does not form a superlative.`}},{id:`b1-mc-230`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`This time next week, we ___ on the beach.`,explanation:`The future continuous shows an action in progress at a future time.`,options:[`will be lying`,`lay`,`lie`,`are lie`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`The present simple does not fit "next week".`,1:`The sentence refers to the future.`,3:`This is not grammatical.`}},{id:`b1-mc-231`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`He ___ me that he had lost his keys.`,explanation:`"Told me that".`,options:[`told`,`talked`,`said`,`spoke`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Said" cannot take a person directly.`,3:`"Speak" does not fit.`,1:`"Talk" does not fit.`}},{id:`b1-mc-232`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"Any" is used after a negative verb.`,options:[`I haven't got a money with me.`,`I haven't got some money with me.`,`I haven't got any money with me.`,`I haven't got no money with me.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`A double negative is incorrect.`,1:`"Some" is used in positive sentences.`,0:`"Money" is uncountable.`}},{id:`b1-mc-233`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`I don't know ___ at the party.`,explanation:`"Anybody" is used after a negative verb.`,options:[`anybody`,`somebody`,`nobody`,`everybody`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Somebody" is used in positive sentences.`,2:`"Nobody" is already negative.`,3:`"Everybody" means all people.`}},{id:`b1-mc-234`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The result of the match depends ___ the weather.`,explanation:`"Depend on" is the fixed combination.`,options:[`on`,`in`,`from`,`of`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Depend of" is not correct.`,2:`"Depend from" is not correct.`,1:`"Depend in" is not correct.`}},{id:`b1-mc-235`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Our car ___ on the way to the airport, so we missed the flight.`,explanation:`"Break down" means to stop working.`,options:[`broke out`,`broke up`,`broke in`,`broke down`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Break up" means to end a relationship.`,0:`"Break out" means to start suddenly.`,2:`"Break in" means to enter illegally.`}},{id:`b1-mc-236`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`The police are trying to ___ out what happened.`,explanation:`"Find out" means to discover.`,options:[`find`,`search`,`look`,`take`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Search out" has a different meaning.`,2:`"Look out" means be careful.`,3:`"Take out" means to remove.`}},{id:`b1-mc-237`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The ___ gave the patient a prescription.`,explanation:`A doctor can prescribe medicine.`,options:[`dentist`,`doctor`,`nurse`,`pharmacist`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`A dentist treats teeth.`,2:`Nurses usually do not prescribe medicine.`,3:`A pharmacist prepares medicines but does not usually prescribe.`}},{id:`b1-mc-238`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`Can you ___ the children while I go shopping?`,explanation:`"Look after" means to take care of.`,options:[`look for`,`look at`,`look after`,`look up`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Look at" means to direct your eyes.`,0:`"Look for" means to search.`,3:`"Look up" means to search in a book.`}},{id:`b1-mc-239`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`She told me ___ interesting story about her grandmother.`,explanation:`"An" is used before a vowel sound.`,options:[`the`,`some`,`a`,`an`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Interesting" begins with a vowel sound.`,0:`"The" would refer to a known story.`,1:`"Some" does not go with a singular noun.`}},{id:`b1-mc-240`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The judge ___ him to two years in prison.`,explanation:`"Sentence someone to" gives a punishment.`,options:[`fined`,`condemned`,`judged`,`sentenced`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Judged" does not take "to two years".`,1:`"Condemned" is much stronger.`,0:`A fine is money, not prison.`}},{id:`b1-mc-241`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`I think you should ___ for the job. It sounds perfect for you.`,explanation:`"Apply for a job" is the standard collocation.`,options:[`demand`,`request`,`ask`,`apply`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Request" is formal and needs an object.`,2:`"Ask for" is used with things.`,0:`"Demand" is too strong.`}},{id:`b1-mc-242`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`I'd like to ___ the table in the restaurant for eight o'clock.`,explanation:`"Book a table" means to reserve it.`,options:[`order`,`buy`,`borrow`,`book`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Borrow" means to take temporarily.`,1:`You do not buy a table in a restaurant.`,0:`You order food, not a table.`}},{id:`b1-mc-243`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Because of the drought, farmers lost most of their ___.`,explanation:`"Crops" are the plants farmers grow for food.`,options:[`crops`,`grains`,`plants`,`seeds`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Plants" is too general.`,3:`"Seeds" are only the beginning of crops.`,1:`"Grains" refers to cereal seeds only.`}},{id:`b1-mc-244`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Please ___ me your phone number so that I can call you later.`,explanation:`"Give me your number" is the standard phrase.`,options:[`say`,`give`,`tell`,`show`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Tell" is for information.`,0:`"Say" cannot take a person directly.`,3:`"Show" is for things you see.`}},{id:`b1-mc-245`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`She sings ___ than anyone else in the choir.`,explanation:`The comparative adverb uses "more".`,options:[`beautifuller`,`more beautifully`,`beautiful`,`most beautifully`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`Adverbs with -ly do not take -er.`,2:`A verb needs an adverb.`,3:`The superlative does not fit "than".`}},{id:`b1-mc-246`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"Afraid of" is the fixed combination.`,options:[`She is afraid of dogs.`,`She is afraid at dogs.`,`She is afraid to dogs.`,`She is afraid from dogs.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Afraid from" is not a correct collocation.`,2:`"Afraid to" is followed by a verb.`,1:`"Afraid at" is not a correct collocation.`}},{id:`b1-mc-247`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`That's the man ___ I borrowed the money.`,explanation:`"From whom" is a formal relative structure.`,options:[`who from`,`where`,`whose`,`from whom`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Who from" is not standard.`,1:`"Where" refers to a place.`,2:`"Whose" shows possession.`}},{id:`b1-mc-248`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`There are ___ people in the queue, so we'll have to wait.`,explanation:`"A lot of" can be used with plural nouns.`,options:[`a`,`much`,`a lot of`,`a little`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Much" is used with uncountable nouns.`,3:`"A little" is used with uncountable nouns.`,0:`"A" cannot go with a plural noun.`}},{id:`b1-mc-249`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`My parents have a lot of friends and they invite ___ every weekend.`,explanation:`"Them" is an object pronoun.`,options:[`them`,`themselves`,`their`,`they`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"They" is a subject pronoun.`,2:`"Their" is a possessive adjective.`,1:`"Themselves" refers back to the subject.`}},{id:`b1-mc-250`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`"You needn't have come." This means:`,explanation:`"Needn't have" means unnecessary action already done.`,options:[`You mustn't come.`,`You can't come.`,`You came, but it wasn't necessary.`,`You didn't come.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Needn't have" means you came.`,0:`This is a prohibition.`,1:`This means impossible.`}},{id:`b1-mc-251`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If I ___ the answer, I would tell you.`,explanation:`"If I knew" is the unreal past form.`,options:[`know`,`would know`,`will know`,`knew`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`The second conditional needs the past simple.`,2:`"Will" is not used in the if-clause.`,1:`"Would" is not used in the if-clause.`}},{id:`b1-mc-252`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`I wish I ___ a car. Then I could drive to work.`,explanation:`"I wish I had" expresses a present wish.`,options:[`would have`,`have`,`will have`,`had`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`After "wish" the verb moves back.`,0:`"Would have" does not express this wish.`,2:`"Will" is not used after "wish".`}},{id:`b1-mc-253`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Please finish your homework ___ Friday.`,explanation:`"By" shows a deadline.`,options:[`until`,`on`,`by`,`at`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Until" shows a continuing action up to a point.`,1:`"On Friday" shows the time of an event.`,3:`"At" is used with clock times.`}},{id:`b1-mc-254`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`We need to ___ the table before the guests arrive.`,explanation:`"Lay the table" means to prepare it.`,options:[`lie`,`lay`,`put on`,`make`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Lie" means to be flat.`,2:`"Put on" is used for clothes.`,3:`"Make the table" is not used.`}},{id:`b1-mc-255`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"As ... as" is used with the base form.`,options:[`She isn't as older than she looks.`,`She isn't as old as she looks.`,`She isn't so older as she looks.`,`She isn't old as she looks.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`After "so ... as" we use the base form.`,0:`"Than" is not used after "as".`,3:`The first "as" is missing.`}},{id:`b1-mc-256`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`"I should have studied more." This means:`,explanation:`"Should have" + past participle expresses regret.`,options:[`I'll study more.`,`I studied a lot.`,`I don't need to study.`,`I didn't study enough.`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`The sentence expresses regret.`,0:`This refers to the future.`,2:`This is not the meaning.`}},{id:`b1-mc-257`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`A friend of ___ told me about this restaurant.`,explanation:`"A friend of mine" uses a possessive pronoun.`,options:[`I`,`mine`,`my`,`me`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Of me" is not used for possession.`,2:`"Of my" is not used without a noun.`,0:`"Of I" is not correct.`}},{id:`b1-mc-258`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`I don't mind which one we choose. ___ of them is fine.`,explanation:`"Either" means one or the other.`,options:[`None`,`Neither`,`Either`,`Both`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Neither" has a negative meaning.`,3:`"Both" would mean we take both.`,0:`"None" is used for more than two.`}},{id:`b1-mc-259`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`We enjoyed ___ at the party.`,explanation:`"Ourselves" is used when subject and object are the same.`,options:[`ourselves`,`we`,`us`,`our`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Us" is not reflexive.`,1:`"We" is a subject pronoun.`,3:`"Our" is a possessive adjective.`}},{id:`b1-mc-260`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`This is the book ___ I told you about.`,explanation:`"That" can introduce a defining relative clause for things.`,options:[`who`,`where`,`that`,`whose`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Who" is used for people, not things.`,1:`"Where" is used for places.`,3:`"Whose" shows possession.`}},{id:`b1-mc-261`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`She is wearing ___ blue dress today.`,explanation:`"A" is used before a consonant sound.`,options:[`the`,`an`,`a`,`–`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Blue" begins with a consonant sound.`,0:`"The" would refer to a dress we already know.`,3:`A singular countable noun needs an article.`}},{id:`b1-mc-262`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`___ unemployment rate has fallen this year.`,explanation:`"The" refers to a specific rate.`,options:[`A`,`The`,`An`,`–`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"A" would mean any rate.`,2:`"Unemployment" begins with a vowel sound but the noun phrase is specific.`,3:`A specific rate needs "the".`}},{id:`b1-mc-263`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If it ___ so late, I would stay longer.`,explanation:`"If it weren't" is the unreal past form.`,options:[`wouldn't be`,`won't be`,`weren't`,`isn't`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`The second conditional needs a past form.`,0:`"Would" is not used in the if-clause.`,1:`"Will" is not used in the if-clause.`}},{id:`b1-mc-264`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`We went to the airport ___ taxi.`,explanation:`"By" is used for means of transport.`,options:[`on`,`with`,`by`,`in`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"With" is not used for means of transport.`,3:`"In" would need "a taxi".`,0:`"On" is used for public transport.`}},{id:`b1-mc-265`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`The shop assistant offered me a ___ because the jacket was damaged.`,explanation:`A discount reduces the price.`,options:[`tip`,`discount`,`refund`,`receipt`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`A refund is money returned after payment.`,3:`A receipt proves payment.`,0:`A tip is money for a service.`}},{id:`b1-mc-266`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`I didn't ___ the meeting because I was ill.`,explanation:`"Attend a meeting" is the standard collocation.`,options:[`assist`,`attend`,`participate`,`visit`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Assist" means to help.`,3:`"Visit" is for places or people.`,2:`"Participate" needs "in".`}},{id:`b1-mc-267`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`What ___ if you saw a ghost?`,explanation:`Second conditional: "would" + base.`,options:[`do you do`,`will you do`,`did you do`,`would you do`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`The if-clause "saw" shows an unreal situation.`,0:`This asks about habit.`,2:`This asks about the past.`}},{id:`b1-mc-268`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Which word means "a short trip to a place and back in the same day"?`,explanation:`An excursion is a short trip.`,options:[`excursion`,`destination`,`souvenir`,`itinerary`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`An itinerary is a plan of a journey.`,2:`A souvenir is an object you buy.`,1:`A destination is where you are going.`}},{id:`b1-mc-269`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Which sentence expresses a prohibition?`,explanation:`"Mustn't" expresses a prohibition.`,options:[`You might use your phone here.`,`You needn't use your phone here.`,`You don't have to use your phone here.`,`You mustn't use your phone here.`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`This means it is not necessary.`,1:`"You needn't use your phone here." does not work here, because this means it is not necessary.`,0:`This is a possibility.`}},{id:`b1-mc-270`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Second conditional: past simple + would.`,options:[`If I would have more time, I would learn to cook.`,`If I have more time, I would learn to cook.`,`If I had more time, I will learn to cook.`,`If I had more time, I would learn to cook.`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Would" is not used in the if-clause.`,1:`This mixes first and second conditional.`,2:`The result clause needs "would".`}},{id:`b1-mc-271`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`First conditional: present simple + will.`,options:[`If it rained, we will stay at home.`,`If it would rain, we will stay at home.`,`If it will rain, we will stay at home.`,`If it rains, we will stay at home.`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Will" is not used in the if-clause.`,0:`This mixes past and future.`,1:`"Would" is not used in the if-clause.`}},{id:`b1-mc-272`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Which word describes water that flows down from a high place in a river?`,explanation:`A waterfall is water dropping from height.`,options:[`waterworks`,`waterproof`,`waterfall`,`watermark`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`Waterproof means not letting water through.`,3:`A watermark is a design on paper.`,0:`Waterworks is a system for water supply.`}},{id:`b1-mc-273`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The ___ of the disease is still unknown.`,explanation:`"Cause" is what makes something happen.`,options:[`effect`,`reason`,`cause`,`result`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Reason" is used for explanations of actions.`,3:`"Result" is the effect.`,0:`"Effect" is the result.`}},{id:`b1-mc-274`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`He has a lot of experience in ___ with children.`,explanation:`"Experience in" + gerund.`,options:[`worked`,`work`,`to work`,`working`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`After a preposition we need a gerund.`,2:`After a preposition we cannot use "to + verb".`,0:`The past form does not follow a preposition.`}},{id:`b1-mc-275`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`She has two sisters, and ___ of them lives in Spain.`,explanation:`"Neither" is used for two people.`,options:[`nobody`,`neither`,`none`,`no one`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"None" is used for more than two.`,3:`"No one" is for people in general.`,0:`"Nobody" is for people in general.`}},{id:`b1-mc-276`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`I'll meet you ___ the entrance of the museum at ten.`,explanation:`"At" is used for a meeting point.`,options:[`on`,`to`,`in`,`at`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"In the entrance" would mean inside.`,0:`"On the entrance" is not used.`,1:`"To" shows direction, not a meeting point.`}},{id:`b1-mc-277`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`My grandmother was born ___ 12 March 1950.`,explanation:`"On" is used with full dates.`,options:[`in`,`by`,`on`,`at`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"In" is used with months and years.`,3:`"At" is used with clock times.`,1:`"By" shows a deadline.`}},{id:`b1-mc-278`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Everything ___ he said was true.`,explanation:`After "everything" we normally use "that".`,options:[`which`,`that`,`who`,`what`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"What" does not follow "everything" as a relative pronoun.`,0:`"Which" is not usual after "everything".`,2:`"Who" is used for people.`}},{id:`b1-mc-279`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`The weather today is ___ than yesterday.`,explanation:`The comparative of "bad" is "worse".`,options:[`badder`,`worst`,`worse`,`bad`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Bad" is the basic form, but a comparison is needed.`,0:`"Bad" has an irregular comparative.`,1:`"Worst" is the superlative.`}},{id:`b1-mc-280`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`He's not very good ___ remembering names.`,explanation:`"Good at" is used for skills.`,options:[`for`,`on`,`in`,`at`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Good in" is not used for skills.`,1:`"Good on" has another meaning.`,0:`"Good for" means beneficial.`}},{id:`b1-mc-281`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`Look! The house ___ right now.`,explanation:`"Is being painted" is the present continuous passive.`,options:[`paints`,`has painted`,`is painted`,`is being painted`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`This does not show an action in progress.`,0:`The house does not paint itself.`,1:`The house does not paint.`}},{id:`b1-mc-282`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`If you want to lose weight, you should ___ out sweets.`,explanation:`"Cut out" means to stop eating or doing something.`,options:[`give`,`take`,`cut`,`put`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Take out" has a different meaning.`,0:`"Give out" means to distribute.`,3:`"Put out" has a different meaning.`}},{id:`b1-mc-283`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`I'm afraid your reservation has been ___. We're fully booked.`,explanation:`"Has been cancelled" is the present perfect passive.`,options:[`cancels`,`cancelling`,`cancelled`,`cancel`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`After "been" we need a participle.`,1:`"cancelling" does not work here, because after "been" we need a participle.`,0:`"cancels" does not work here, because after "been" we need a participle.`}},{id:`b1-mc-284`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`They finally agreed ___ a date for the wedding.`,explanation:`"Agree on" is used for reaching a decision together.`,options:[`on`,`with`,`at`,`to`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Agree with" is used before a person.`,3:`"Agree to" is used before a proposal.`,2:`"Agree at" is not correct.`}},{id:`b1-mc-285`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`___ of my friends live in the same street as me.`,explanation:`"Both" is used when we talk about two people.`,options:[`Each`,`Both`,`Every`,`All`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Every" is followed by a singular noun.`,0:`"Each" is followed by a singular noun or "of" plus a plural noun.`,3:`"All of my friends" suggests more than two.`}},{id:`b1-mc-286`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`He opened the box ___ a screwdriver.`,explanation:`"With" introduces an instrument.`,options:[`using`,`in`,`by`,`with`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"By" introduces a way or a doer.`,0:`"Using" is a participle.`,1:`"In" does not show an instrument.`}},{id:`b1-mc-287`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The plane flew ___ the clouds.`,explanation:`"Above" means at a higher level.`,options:[`between`,`beside`,`above`,`among`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Between" needs two things.`,1:`"Beside" means next to.`,3:`"Among" is used with several things.`}},{id:`b1-mc-288`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Volunteers are people who work without being ___.`,explanation:`"Paid" is the past participle.`,options:[`paid`,`payed`,`paying`,`pay`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`The spelling is "paid".`,3:`After "being" use the participle.`,2:`"Being paying" is not correct.`}},{id:`b1-mc-289`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`I'll lend you the book ___ you give it back tomorrow.`,explanation:`"Provided" means "on condition that".`,options:[`because`,`unless`,`provided`,`although`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Although" shows contrast.`,1:`"Unless" has the opposite meaning.`,0:`"Because" gives a reason.`}},{id:`b1-mc-290`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`I can't find my glasses. I think I ___ them on the bus.`,explanation:`"Left" is used with a place.`,options:[`lost`,`forgot`,`missed`,`left`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Forgot" needs a clause.`,0:`"Lost" means you do not know where they are.`,2:`"Missed" means failed to catch.`}},{id:`b1-mc-291`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`He has a very high ___ and earns a lot of money every month.`,explanation:`A salary is a regular payment for work.`,options:[`salary`,`solitary`,`sailor`,`salad`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`A sailor works on a ship.`,3:`A salad is a dish of vegetables.`,1:`"Solitary" means alone.`}},{id:`b1-mc-292`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`I've got ___ time, so I can help you for a few minutes.`,explanation:`"A little" goes with uncountable nouns.`,options:[`a little`,`few`,`a few`,`many`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"A few" is used with plural countable nouns.`,3:`"Many" is used with plural countable nouns.`,1:`"Few" has a negative meaning and goes with countable nouns.`}},{id:`b1-mc-293`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The man ___ lives next door is a pilot.`,explanation:`"Who" is the relative pronoun for people.`,options:[`which`,`whose`,`who`,`where`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Which" is used for things, not people.`,1:`"Whose" shows possession, not the subject.`,3:`"Where" is used for places.`}},{id:`b1-mc-294`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`This sweater ___ of pure wool.`,explanation:`"Is made of" is the present passive.`,options:[`made`,`is made`,`is making`,`makes`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`The sweater does not make.`,2:`"is making" does not work here, because the sweater does not make.`,0:`"Is" is missing.`}},{id:`b1-mc-295`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`He apologised ___ being late for the meeting.`,explanation:`"Apologise for" is followed by the reason.`,options:[`for`,`of`,`about`,`to`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Apologise of" is not a correct collocation.`,2:`"Apologise about" is much less common.`,3:`"Apologise to" is followed by a person.`}},{id:`b1-mc-296`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`My brother wants to be ___ engineer when he grows up.`,explanation:`Before a vowel sound we use "an": an engineer.`,options:[`–`,`an`,`the`,`a`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Engineer" begins with a vowel sound, so "a" does not fit.`,2:`"The" would refer to a specific, known engineer.`,0:`A singular job title needs an article here.`}},{id:`b1-mc-297`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`She's very happy ___ her new flat.`,explanation:`"Happy with" is the usual combination.`,options:[`for`,`of`,`with`,`at`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Happy of" is not correct.`,3:`"Happy at" is not the usual combination.`,0:`"Happy for" refers to feeling pleased for someone.`}},{id:`b1-mc-298`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`We went to ___ cinema last night.`,explanation:`We say "go to the cinema" for the place.`,options:[`a`,`an`,`the`,`–`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"A cinema" would suggest any cinema, but the expression is fixed.`,3:`"Go to cinema" is not used in this sense.`,1:`"Cinema" begins with a consonant sound.`}},{id:`b1-mc-299`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The beach was so ___ that we could hardly find a place to sit.`,explanation:`"Crowded" means full of people.`,options:[`filled`,`full up`,`crowded`,`busy up`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Busy up" is not used.`,1:`"Full up" is informal.`,0:`"Filled" is not the usual adjective.`}},{id:`b1-mc-300`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`He plays ___ tennis every weekend.`,explanation:`We use no article with sports.`,options:[`a`,`–`,`the`,`an`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`With sports we do not use "the".`,0:`With sports we do not use "a".`,3:`With sports we do not use "an".`}},{id:`b1-mc-301`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`I'll never forget the day ___ we met.`,explanation:`"When" refers to a time.`,options:[`where`,`when`,`which`,`who`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Where" refers to a place.`,2:`"Which" would need a preposition.`,3:`"Who" refers to people.`}},{id:`b1-mc-302`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`I don't like either of ___ shirts. They are both too big.`,explanation:`"These" is the plural demonstrative.`,options:[`this`,`a`,`these`,`the other`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"This" is singular, but "shirts" is plural.`,1:`"A" cannot be used with a plural noun.`,3:`"The other" does not fit here.`}},{id:`b1-mc-303`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`___ knows the answer. It's too difficult.`,explanation:`"Nobody" means no person.`,options:[`Nobody`,`Somebody`,`Anybody`,`Everybody`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Anybody" needs a negative verb.`,1:`"Somebody" would mean at least one person knows.`,3:`"Everybody" would mean all people know.`}},{id:`b1-mc-304`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`A ___ is someone who sees a crime happen.`,explanation:`A witness sees an event.`,options:[`suspect`,`witness`,`victim`,`lawyer`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`A victim suffers from the crime.`,0:`A suspect is thought to have committed it.`,3:`A lawyer gives legal advice.`}},{id:`b1-mc-305`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`She has ___ finished her homework. She's watching TV now.`,explanation:`"Just" means a short time ago.`,options:[`yet`,`just`,`still`,`ever`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Yet" is used in questions and negatives.`,2:`"Still" does not fit a completed action.`,3:`"Ever" is used in questions and negatives.`}},{id:`b1-mc-306`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`When I got home, my parents ___ dinner.`,explanation:`The past continuous shows an action in progress.`,options:[`had`,`have`,`are having`,`were having`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`The longer action needs the past continuous.`,1:`The sentence is in the past.`,2:`"are having" does not work here, because the sentence is in the past.`}},{id:`b1-mc-307`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The man ___ sat next to me on the plane was a pilot.`,explanation:`"Who" refers to people.`,options:[`whose`,`who`,`which`,`where`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Which" is used for things.`,0:`"Whose" shows possession.`,3:`"Where" refers to a place.`}},{id:`b1-mc-308`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If she had known, she ___ come.`,explanation:`Third conditional: would have + participle.`,options:[`had`,`will`,`would have`,`would`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`A third conditional needs "would have".`,1:`"Will" does not fit an unreal past.`,0:`"Had" is the if-clause form.`}},{id:`b1-mc-309`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"Since" is followed by a point in time.`,options:[`We have lived here in 2010.`,`We have lived here for 2010.`,`We have lived here from 2010.`,`We have lived here since 2010.`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"For" is followed by a length of time.`,2:`"From" needs an end point.`,0:`"In" does not show duration up to now.`}},{id:`b1-mc-310`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`She has a lot of ___ in teaching, having worked in schools for twenty years.`,explanation:`"Experience" is the knowledge gained from doing something.`,options:[`experiment`,`expertise`,`experience`,`experienced`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`An experiment is a scientific test.`,3:`"Experienced" is an adjective, not a noun.`,1:`"Expertise" means special skill and is less natural here.`}},{id:`b1-mc-311`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`I always have ___ breakfast at seven.`,explanation:`We use no article with meals: have breakfast.`,options:[`–`,`a`,`an`,`the`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`With meals we do not usually use "the".`,1:`With meals in general we do not use "a".`,2:`With meals in general we do not use "an".`}},{id:`b1-mc-312`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`She has a lot of ___ in her new job.`,explanation:`"Responsibility" is the noun.`,options:[`responsibility`,`responsible`,`responsibilities of`,`responsibly`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Responsible" is an adjective.`,3:`"Responsibly" is an adverb.`,2:`"Of" does not fit here.`}},{id:`b1-mc-313`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Look at ___ over there! It's amazing!`,explanation:`"That" refers to something at a distance.`,options:[`them`,`these`,`those`,`that`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Those" would need a plural noun.`,1:`"These" refers to things nearby.`,0:`"Them" would need a plural meaning.`}},{id:`b1-mc-314`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Is this pen ___ or is it Tom's?`,explanation:`"Hers" is a possessive pronoun that stands alone.`,options:[`herself`,`hers`,`her`,`she`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Her" must be followed by a noun.`,3:`"She" is a subject pronoun.`,0:`"Herself" is reflexive.`}},{id:`b1-mc-315`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`We have run ___ of milk. Can you buy some?`,explanation:`"Run out of" means to have none left.`,options:[`out`,`off`,`up`,`in`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Run off" means to flee.`,2:`"Run up" means to increase a bill.`,3:`"Run in" has a different meaning.`}},{id:`b1-mc-316`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`He is allergic ___ nuts.`,explanation:`"Allergic to".`,options:[`of`,`at`,`to`,`with`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Allergic at" is not correct.`,0:`"Allergic of" is not correct.`,3:`"Allergic with" is not correct.`}},{id:`b1-mc-317`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`I'm looking for a flat to ___. My contract ends next month.`,explanation:`"Rent" is used for accommodation.`,options:[`borrow`,`rent`,`hire`,`lend`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Lend" is to give temporarily.`,2:`"Hire" is for cars or equipment.`,0:`"Borrow" is to take temporarily.`}},{id:`b1-mc-318`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`It's ___ cold to go swimming today.`,explanation:`"Too" means more than is acceptable.`,options:[`enough`,`very`,`so`,`too`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Enough" goes after the adjective.`,1:`"Very" does not express a problem.`,2:`"So" does not express a problem here.`}},{id:`b1-mc-319`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`She was accused ___ stealing money from the shop.`,explanation:`"Accuse someone of" is the fixed combination.`,options:[`of`,`for`,`with`,`about`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Accused for" is not correct.`,2:`"Accused with" is not correct.`,3:`"Accused about" is not correct.`}},{id:`b1-mc-320`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I ___ for you since six o'clock. Where were you?`,explanation:`The present perfect continuous stresses duration.`,options:[`waited`,`am waiting`,`have been waiting`,`was waiting`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Since" needs the present perfect.`,1:`The present continuous does not show duration.`,3:`The sentence refers to now.`}},{id:`b1-mc-321`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`After a long journey we were glad to ___ our luggage in the room.`,explanation:`"Unpack" means to take things out of bags.`,options:[`undo`,`unload up`,`unpack`,`uncover`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Uncover" means to remove a cover.`,0:`"Undo" means to reverse.`,1:`"Unload up" is not used.`}},{id:`b1-mc-322`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Which sentence expresses a deduction?`,explanation:`"Must be" expresses a strong deduction.`,options:[`He has to be tired after the trip.`,`He must be tired after the trip.`,`He should be tired after the trip.`,`He can be tired after the trip.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Should" expresses expectation.`,0:`"Has to" expresses obligation.`,3:`"Can" expresses general possibility.`}},{id:`b1-mc-323`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`The past perfect shows an earlier action.`,options:[`By the time we arrived, the film started.`,`By the time we arrived, the film was start.`,`By the time we arrived, the film had started.`,`By the time we arrived, the film has started.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`An earlier action calls for the past perfect.`,3:`The present perfect does not fit a past story.`,1:`This is not grammatical.`}},{id:`b1-mc-324`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Of the three hotels, this one is the ___ expensive.`,explanation:`"The least" is the superlative of "little".`,options:[`less`,`fewer`,`little`,`least`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Less" compares only two.`,2:`"Little" is not used here.`,1:`"Fewer" is used with countable nouns.`}},{id:`b1-mc-325`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`I need ___ advice about my studies.`,explanation:`"Some" can be used with uncountable nouns.`,options:[`an`,`some`,`a`,`many`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Advice" is uncountable, so "an" is wrong.`,2:`"Advice" is uncountable, so "a" is wrong.`,3:`"Many" is used with plural countable nouns.`}},{id:`b1-mc-326`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`When he heard the joke, he burst out ___.`,explanation:`"Burst out laughing" is the fixed expression.`,options:[`to laugh`,`laughed`,`laughing`,`laugh`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Burst out" is followed by a gerund.`,3:`The base form does not follow "burst out".`,1:`The past form does not follow "burst out".`}},{id:`b1-mc-327`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`We use "on" with days and "at" with clock times.`,options:[`The meeting is on Tuesday at ten.`,`The meeting is in Tuesday at ten.`,`The meeting is on Tuesday in ten.`,`The meeting is at Tuesday on ten.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"In" is not used with days.`,3:`Both prepositions are in the wrong place.`,2:`"In" is not used with clock times.`}},{id:`b1-mc-328`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`I'm very interested ___ learning more about photography.`,explanation:`"Interested in" is the fixed expression.`,options:[`in`,`about`,`at`,`on`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Interested on" is not a correct collocation.`,2:`"Interested at" is not a correct collocation.`,1:`"Interested about" is not the standard combination.`}},{id:`b1-mc-329`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If I ___ the train, I'd have been late.`,explanation:`"Had missed" is the past perfect.`,options:[`missed`,`miss`,`had missed`,`would miss`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`A third conditional needs the past perfect.`,3:`"Would" is not used in the if-clause.`,1:`The present does not fit a past event.`}},{id:`b1-mc-330`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`A reported yes/no question uses "if" or "whether".`,options:[`He asked me whether I could help him.`,`He asked me could I help him.`,`He asked me that I could help him.`,`He asked me whether can I help him.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`A reported question uses "if" or "whether".`,2:`"That" does not introduce a question.`,3:`Reported questions use normal word order.`}},{id:`b1-mc-331`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`This laptop is ___ expensive than that one.`,explanation:`"Less" is the comparative of "little".`,options:[`little`,`fewer`,`less`,`least`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Least" is the superlative.`,1:`"Fewer" is used with countable nouns.`,0:`"Little" is not a comparative.`}},{id:`b1-mc-332`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`I was reading ___ the train journey.`,explanation:`"During" is followed by a noun.`,options:[`for`,`while`,`during`,`since`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"For" is followed by a length of time.`,1:`"While" is followed by a clause.`,3:`"Since" starts at a point in the past.`}},{id:`b1-mc-333`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`It was ___ a good book that I read it twice.`,explanation:`"Such a" + adjective + noun expresses a result.`,options:[`such`,`too`,`so`,`very`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"So" is followed directly by an adjective.`,1:`"Too" does not express a result.`,3:`"Very" does not express a result.`}},{id:`b1-mc-334`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`We've lived here ___ 2012.`,explanation:`"Since" is followed by a point in time.`,options:[`since`,`from`,`for`,`in`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"For" is followed by a period, not a point.`,1:`"From" needs an end point.`,3:`"In" does not show duration up to now.`}},{id:`b1-mc-335`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`She's very ___ in her opinions and never changes her mind.`,explanation:`"Stubborn" means refusing to change your mind.`,options:[`strict`,`stable`,`strong`,`stubborn`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Stable" means steady and not likely to change.`,0:`"Strict" refers to firm rules.`,2:`"Strong" refers to power.`}},{id:`b1-mc-336`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`Which sentence is passive?`,explanation:`A passive sentence has a form of "be" + past participle.`,options:[`The children ate the cake.`,`The cake was eaten by the children.`,`The children have eaten the cake.`,`The children are eating the cake.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`This is active because the children do the action.`,3:`This is active and in the continuous.`,2:`This is active in the present perfect.`}},{id:`b1-mc-337`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`I like both bags. I'll take ___ of them.`,explanation:`"Either" means one or the other.`,options:[`none`,`neither`,`each`,`either`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Neither" has a negative meaning.`,0:`"None" is used for more than two.`,2:`"Each" is followed by "of" and a plural noun only in different structures.`}},{id:`b1-mc-338`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`I'm trying to ___ smoking, but it's very difficult.`,explanation:`"Give up" means to stop a habit.`,options:[`give up`,`give away`,`give in`,`give out`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Give in" means to surrender.`,3:`"Give out" means to distribute.`,1:`"Give away" means to offer for free.`}},{id:`b1-mc-339`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Your English is getting ___ every day.`,explanation:`"Better and better" shows gradual improvement.`,options:[`more and more good`,`best and best`,`good and good`,`better and better`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`The comparative is needed.`,0:`"Good" has an irregular comparative.`,1:`The superlative does not fit.`}},{id:`b1-mc-340`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Could you tell me where the ___ is? I need to catch the 9.30 train.`,explanation:`"Platform" is where passengers wait for trains.`,options:[`pavement`,`station hall`,`platform`,`passage`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`A pavement is a path for walkers.`,3:`A passage is a narrow way.`,1:`This is not the exact place.`}},{id:`b1-mc-341`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`The past perfect shows the earlier event.`,options:[`The train had left before we arrived.`,`The train has left before we arrived.`,`The train was leaving before we arrived.`,`The train left before we had arrived.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`The present perfect does not fit a past story.`,3:`The earlier event should have the past perfect.`,2:`The past continuous does not stress completion.`}},{id:`b1-mc-342`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The woman ___ I met at the conference is a doctor.`,explanation:`"Whom" is an object relative pronoun for people.`,options:[`where`,`which`,`whose`,`whom`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Which" is used for things.`,2:`"Whose" shows possession.`,0:`"Where" refers to a place.`}},{id:`b1-mc-343`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The company is famous ___ its excellent customer service.`,explanation:`"Famous for" gives the reason for fame.`,options:[`with`,`of`,`to`,`for`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Famous of" is not correct.`,0:`"Famous with" is not correct.`,2:`"Famous to" is not correct.`}},{id:`b1-mc-344`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`What do you use to dry yourself after a shower?`,explanation:`A towel dries your body.`,options:[`towel`,`tissue`,`apron`,`blanket`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`A tissue is for wiping the nose.`,2:`An apron protects clothes in cooking.`,3:`A blanket keeps you warm.`}},{id:`b1-mc-345`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`I think you ___ talk to your teacher about the problem.`,explanation:`"Should" gives advice.`,options:[`needn't`,`can't`,`should`,`mustn't`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Can't" means it is impossible.`,3:`"Mustn't" is a prohibition.`,0:`"Needn't" removes the advice.`}},{id:`b1-mc-346`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The earthquake ___ a lot of damage in the area.`,explanation:`"Cause damage" is the standard collocation.`,options:[`caused`,`made`,`did`,`had`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Make damage" is not the usual collocation.`,2:`"Do damage" is possible but "cause" is the usual verb.`,3:`"Have damage" is not the usual collocation.`}},{id:`b1-mc-347`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"Both" takes a plural verb.`,options:[`Both of my parents works in a hospital.`,`Both of my parent work in a hospital.`,`Both my parents is work in a hospital.`,`Both of my parents work in a hospital.`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Both" takes a plural verb.`,2:`The verb phrase is not grammatical.`,1:`"Parents" must be plural.`}},{id:`b1-mc-348`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"Married to" is the standard combination.`,options:[`She is married at a doctor.`,`She is married by a doctor.`,`She is married with a doctor.`,`She is married to a doctor.`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Married with" is not standard English.`,1:`"Married by" means a doctor performed the wedding.`,0:`"Married at" is not correct.`}},{id:`b1-mc-349`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`What do we call the person who writes computer programs?`,explanation:`A programmer writes code.`,options:[`printer`,`professor`,`presenter`,`programmer`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`A printer is a machine.`,2:`A presenter introduces shows.`,1:`A professor teaches at university.`}},{id:`b1-mc-350`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`We need to ___ a decision about the holiday before Friday.`,explanation:`"Make a decision" is the standard collocation.`,options:[`have`,`make`,`take on`,`do`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Do a decision" is not a standard collocation in English.`,2:`"Take on" means to accept a responsibility.`,0:`"Have a decision" does not mean to decide.`}},{id:`b1-mc-351`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`She speaks English ___ for a beginner.`,explanation:`"Quite well" modifies the verb "speaks".`,options:[`quite good`,`much good`,`quite well`,`well quite`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`A verb needs an adverb.`,3:`The word order is incorrect.`,1:`"Much good" has a different meaning.`}},{id:`b1-mc-352`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`___ student in the class has to write an essay.`,explanation:`"Every" is followed by a singular noun.`,options:[`Both`,`Some`,`All`,`Every`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"All" is followed by a plural noun.`,0:`"Both" is used for two people only.`,1:`"Some" does not mean all students.`}},{id:`b1-mc-353`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Visitors are not ___ to take photographs inside the museum.`,explanation:`"Are not allowed to" is the passive form.`,options:[`let`,`allowed`,`agreed`,`permitted to`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Permitted to" would repeat "to".`,0:`"Let" does not take a passive.`,2:`"Agreed" does not fit.`}},{id:`b1-mc-354`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`These aren't my glasses. Are they ___?`,explanation:`"Yours" is a possessive pronoun that stands alone.`,options:[`yourself`,`your`,`you`,`yours`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Your" must be followed by a noun.`,2:`"You" is a subject pronoun.`,0:`"Yourself" is reflexive.`}},{id:`b1-mc-355`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`The book was ___ interesting that I couldn't put it down.`,explanation:`"So" + adjective expresses a result.`,options:[`very`,`so`,`too`,`such`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Such" is followed by a noun phrase.`,2:`"Too" does not express a result.`,0:`"Very" does not express a result.`}},{id:`b1-mc-356`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"Was passed" is the past passive.`,options:[`The law has passed last year.`,`The law is passed last year.`,`The law was passed last year.`,`The law passed last year.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`A law cannot pass itself.`,1:`"Last year" needs the past.`,0:`"Last year" is a finished time.`}},{id:`b1-mc-357`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Excuse me, I'm looking ___ the post office.`,explanation:`"Look for" means to search.`,options:[`for`,`up`,`after`,`at`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Look at" means to direct your eyes.`,2:`"Look after" means to take care of.`,1:`"Look up" means to search in a book.`}},{id:`b1-mc-358`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`A person who writes computer programs is called a ___.`,explanation:`A programmer writes code.`,options:[`presenter`,`producer`,`professor`,`programmer`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`A professor teaches at university.`,1:`A producer organises media production.`,0:`A presenter introduces shows.`}},{id:`b1-mc-359`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`She's applying ___ a job as a teacher.`,explanation:`"Apply for a job".`,options:[`to`,`on`,`at`,`for`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Apply to" is followed by an organisation.`,2:`"Apply at" is not used for a position.`,1:`"Apply on" is not correct.`}},{id:`b1-mc-360`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`He was rushed to hospital with a serious ___.`,explanation:`"Injury" is damage from an accident.`,options:[`disease`,`wound`,`injury`,`illness`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`An illness is caused by disease, not an accident.`,1:`A wound is possible but "serious injury" is the usual collocation.`,0:`A disease is an illness.`}},{id:`b1-mc-361`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`He's very ___ about his appearance and always checks the mirror.`,explanation:`"Vain" means too proud of one's looks.`,options:[`vain`,`vein`,`vague`,`vane`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`A vein is a blood vessel.`,3:`A vane is a weather instrument.`,2:`"Vague" means unclear.`}},{id:`b1-mc-362`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`This computer is ___ than my old one.`,explanation:`The comparative of "fast" is "faster".`,options:[`fast`,`more fast`,`fastest`,`faster`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`A comparison with "than" needs a comparative form.`,2:`The superlative does not fit "than".`,1:`Short adjectives take -er.`}},{id:`b1-mc-363`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`She had to ___ a few days off work because she was ill.`,explanation:`"Take time off" is the usual collocation.`,options:[`take`,`make`,`do`,`put`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Make" does not collocate with days off.`,2:`"Do" does not collocate with days off.`,3:`"Put" does not collocate with days off.`}},{id:`b1-mc-364`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`I can't ___ why he left without saying goodbye.`,explanation:`"Understand" means to get the meaning.`,options:[`stand`,`underline`,`understand`,`undertake`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`It means tolerate or be upright.`,1:`It means to draw a line under.`,3:`It means to begin a task.`}},{id:`b1-mc-365`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`Our hotel room ___ every day.`,explanation:`"Is cleaned" is the present passive.`,options:[`has cleaned`,`is cleaned`,`cleans`,`cleaning`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`The room does not clean.`,3:`"Cleaning" is not a complete passive.`,0:`"has cleaned" does not work here, because the room does not clean.`}},{id:`b1-mc-366`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Remember to ___ your passport before you leave the hotel.`,explanation:`"Take" is used for leaving.`,options:[`carry on`,`bring`,`wear`,`take`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Bring" is towards the speaker.`,0:`"Carry on" means continue.`,2:`You do not wear a passport.`}},{id:`b1-mc-367`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`She came ___ of the building with a large bag.`,explanation:`"Out of" shows leaving an enclosed space.`,options:[`off`,`out`,`away`,`from`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Off" is used for a surface.`,3:`"From" does not fit with "of".`,2:`"Away" does not fit with "of".`}},{id:`b1-mc-368`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`I usually go to work ___ bus.`,explanation:`We say "by bus" for means of transport.`,options:[`by`,`in`,`with`,`on`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"In bus" is not used for general transport.`,3:`"On bus" is not used for general transport.`,2:`"With" is not used for transport.`}},{id:`b1-mc-369`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The meeting has been ___ until next Thursday because the manager is ill.`,explanation:`"Postponed" means moved to a later time.`,options:[`postponed`,`prepared`,`proposed`,`prevented`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Prevented" means stopped from happening.`,2:`"Proposed" means suggested.`,1:`"Prepared" means made ready.`}},{id:`b1-mc-370`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"Advice" is uncountable.`,options:[`I need a few advice about my studies.`,`I need an advice about my studies.`,`I need advices about my studies.`,`I need some advice about my studies.`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Advice" is uncountable and cannot take "an".`,2:`"Advice" has no plural form.`,0:`"A few" is used with plural nouns.`}},{id:`b1-mc-371`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"Herself" is used when subject and object are the same.`,options:[`She hurt hers while she was playing tennis.`,`She hurt her while she was playing tennis.`,`She hurt she while she was playing tennis.`,`She hurt herself while she was playing tennis.`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Her" refers to another person.`,0:`"Hers" is a possessive pronoun.`,2:`"She" is a subject pronoun.`}},{id:`b1-mc-372`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`You ___ park here. It's a hospital entrance.`,explanation:`"Mustn't" expresses a prohibition.`,options:[`could`,`may`,`needn't`,`mustn't`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Needn't" means it is not necessary.`,1:`"May" would give permission.`,0:`"Could" expresses possibility.`}},{id:`b1-mc-373`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Which of these is a type of accommodation?`,explanation:`A hostel is cheap accommodation.`,options:[`highway`,`hostel`,`harbour`,`horizon`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`A harbour is a port for ships.`,0:`A highway is a main road.`,3:`The horizon is where sky meets earth.`}},{id:`b1-mc-374`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Which word means "the money you earn from your job each month"?`,explanation:`"Salary" is a fixed monthly payment.`,options:[`wage`,`salary`,`pension`,`tax`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Wage" is usually paid weekly or hourly.`,2:`A pension is money after retirement.`,3:`Tax is money paid to the government.`}},{id:`b1-mc-375`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Has the train arrived ___ ?`,explanation:`"Yet" is used in questions.`,options:[`just`,`still`,`already`,`yet`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Already" is used in positive sentences.`,1:`"Still" does not fit the question.`,0:`"Just" would be placed before the participle.`}},{id:`b1-mc-376`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`She is the person who is responsible ___ the whole project.`,explanation:`"Responsible for" is the fixed combination.`,options:[`about`,`of`,`for`,`to`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Responsible of" is not correct.`,3:`"Responsible to" is followed by a boss.`,0:`"Responsible about" is not correct.`}},{id:`b1-mc-377`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`He is ___ honest man who always tells the truth.`,explanation:`"An" is used before a vowel sound.`,options:[`the`,`a`,`an`,`–`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Honest" begins with a vowel sound.`,0:`"The" would refer to a known man.`,3:`A singular noun needs an article.`}},{id:`b1-mc-378`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`She suggested ___ to the new restaurant.`,explanation:`"Suggest" + gerund.`,options:[`go`,`went`,`going`,`to go`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Suggest" is followed by a gerund.`,0:`The base form does not follow "suggest".`,1:`The past form does not follow "suggest".`}},{id:`b1-mc-379`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`I like ___ of these pictures. They're both beautiful.`,explanation:`"Both" is used for two things.`,options:[`all`,`either`,`both`,`each`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"All" is used for more than two.`,3:`"Each" is followed by a singular noun.`,1:`"Either" means one of two.`}},{id:`b1-mc-380`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`She is the only person ___ I can trust.`,explanation:`"That" can follow "only".`,options:[`whose`,`that`,`which`,`who's`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Who's" means "who is".`,0:`"Whose" shows possession.`,2:`"Which" is used for things.`}},{id:`b1-mc-381`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`The beach was ___ than we expected.`,explanation:`The comparative of "clean" is "cleaner".`,options:[`more clean`,`cleaner`,`clean`,`cleanest`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`A comparison with "than" needs a comparative form.`,3:`The superlative does not fit "than".`,0:`Short adjectives take -er.`}},{id:`b1-mc-382`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"Faster" is the comparative of "fast".`,options:[`He drives more fast than his brother.`,`He drives fastly than his brother.`,`He drives as faster as his brother.`,`He drives faster than his brother.`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`Short adverbs take -er.`,1:`"Fast" has no -ly form.`,2:`"As ... as" uses the base form.`}},{id:`b1-mc-383`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The report must be ___ by tomorrow morning.`,explanation:`"Be finished" is passive.`,options:[`finished`,`finishing`,`finish`,`finishes`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`After "be" use a past participle.`,1:`"Be finishing" does not make a passive.`,3:`It is not a passive form.`}},{id:`b1-mc-384`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`Long adjectives form the superlative with "most".`,options:[`This is the most beautiful place I have ever seen.`,`This is the more beautiful place I have ever seen.`,`This is the beautifulest place I have ever seen.`,`This is the most beautifulest place I have ever seen.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`Long adjectives do not take -est as well as "most".`,1:`"More" compares only two.`,2:`Long adjectives take "most".`}},{id:`b1-mc-385`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`After ten years in the same company, he decided to ___.`,explanation:`"Resign" means to leave a job formally.`,options:[`relax`,`resign`,`retire`,`rely`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Retire" is due to age.`,0:`"Relax" means to rest.`,3:`"Rely" means to depend.`}},{id:`b1-mc-386`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The police ___ two men for the robbery.`,explanation:`"Arrest" means to take someone into custody.`,options:[`caught up`,`blamed`,`arrested`,`detained up`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Caught up" has a different meaning.`,3:`"Detained up" is not correct.`,1:`"Blamed" is not the legal action.`}},{id:`b1-mc-387`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`It's ___ 10 a.m., and she's still asleep!`,explanation:`"Already" expresses that something has happened earlier than expected.`,options:[`just`,`already`,`yet`,`still`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Yet" is used in questions and negatives.`,3:`"Still" does not express surprise at earliness.`,0:`"Just" means a moment ago.`}},{id:`b1-mc-388`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`I haven't seen ___ of them for years.`,explanation:`"Any of" is used after a negative verb.`,options:[`no`,`some`,`any`,`every`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Some" is used in positive sentences.`,0:`"No" cannot be followed by "of".`,3:`"Every" cannot be followed by "of".`}},{id:`b1-mc-389`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Everybody ___ happy when they heard the news.`,explanation:`"Everybody" takes a singular verb.`,options:[`was`,`are`,`have been`,`were`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Everybody" takes a singular verb.`,1:`The sentence is in the past.`,2:`"have been" does not work here, because "Everybody" takes a singular verb.`}},{id:`b1-mc-390`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`"Where have you been?" she asked. → She asked me where I ___.`,explanation:`The present perfect becomes the past perfect.`,options:[`been`,`was`,`had been`,`have been`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`Reported questions move back in time.`,1:`"Have been" becomes "had been".`,0:`A finite verb is needed.`}},{id:`b1-mc-391`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Many species are in danger of ___.`,explanation:`"Extinction" means disappearing forever.`,options:[`exhaustion`,`extinction`,`extension`,`extraction`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Extension" means making longer.`,0:`"Exhaustion" means extreme tiredness.`,3:`"Extraction" means taking out.`}},{id:`b1-mc-392`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`How ___ homework do you have tonight?`,explanation:`"How much" is used with uncountable nouns.`,options:[`many`,`few`,`a lot`,`much`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Homework" is uncountable, so "many" is wrong.`,1:`"Few" is used with plural countable nouns.`,2:`"A lot" needs "of" in this position.`}},{id:`b1-mc-393`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`The hotel was far ___ than we expected, and the room was beautiful.`,explanation:`"Better" is the comparative of "good".`,options:[`good`,`better`,`best`,`well`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`A comparison with "than" needs a comparative form.`,2:`The superlative does not fit "than".`,3:`"Well" is an adverb and cannot describe the hotel.`}},{id:`b1-mc-394`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Wind and solar are examples of ___ energy.`,explanation:`"Renewable energy" comes from sources that do not run out.`,options:[`renewable`,`reusable`,`recyclable`,`replaceable`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Reusable" refers to objects.`,3:`"Replaceable" has a different meaning.`,2:`"Recyclable" refers to materials.`}},{id:`b1-mc-395`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"For" with duration up to now uses the present perfect.`,options:[`She worked here for six years now.`,`She has been working here for six years.`,`She is working here for six years.`,`She works here since six years.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"For six years" needs a perfect form.`,3:`"Since" is followed by a point in time.`,0:`"Now" does not fit the simple past.`}},{id:`b1-mc-396`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"Little" is used with uncountable nouns.`,options:[`She has very a little money.`,`She has very few money.`,`She has very little money.`,`She has very much money little.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Few" is used with plural countable nouns.`,0:`"Very a little" is not correct.`,3:`The word order is incorrect.`}},{id:`b1-mc-397`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The house ___ roof was damaged is being repaired.`,explanation:`"Whose" shows possession in a relative clause.`,options:[`whose`,`which`,`who`,`that`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Which" would need "the roof of which".`,3:`"That" does not show possession.`,2:`"Who" is used for people.`}},{id:`b1-mc-398`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`"I can't swim," she said. → She said that she ___.`,explanation:`"Can't" becomes "couldn't".`,options:[`can't swim`,`wouldn't swim`,`couldn't swim`,`didn't swim`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`In reported speech "can't" usually becomes "couldn't".`,3:`This changes the meaning.`,1:`"Wouldn't" does not mean inability.`}},{id:`b1-mc-399`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Excuse me, can I ___ this printer? It isn't working.`,explanation:`"Use" fits machines.`,options:[`use`,`wear`,`play`,`spend`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`You do not wear a printer.`,2:`You do not play a printer.`,3:`"Spend" is used with time or money.`}},{id:`b1-mc-400`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`___ of my friends live in London.`,explanation:`"Some of" is used with plural nouns.`,options:[`Some`,`A`,`Any`,`Much`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Any" is used in negatives and questions.`,1:`"A" cannot go with a plural verb.`,3:`"Much" is used with uncountable nouns.`}},{id:`b1-mc-401`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`That is the hotel ___ we stayed last summer.`,explanation:`"Where" refers to a place.`,options:[`when`,`who`,`where`,`which`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Which" would need a preposition ("in which").`,1:`"Who" is used for people.`,0:`"When" refers to time.`}},{id:`b1-mc-402`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`They gave us ___ to eat. We were starving.`,explanation:`"Nothing" fits "we were starving".`,options:[`nothing`,`anything`,`everything`,`something`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Anything" needs a negative verb.`,3:`"Something" would mean they gave food.`,2:`"Everything" would mean all the food.`}},{id:`b1-mc-403`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`He's ___ tallest boy in our class.`,explanation:`Superlatives take "the".`,options:[`an`,`–`,`a`,`the`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"A" cannot go with a superlative.`,0:`"An" cannot go with a superlative.`,1:`A superlative needs "the".`}},{id:`b1-mc-404`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`My brother has a very ___ job: he is a firefighter.`,explanation:`The adjective "dangerous" describes the job.`,options:[`endangered`,`dangerous`,`dangerously`,`danger`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Danger" is a noun and cannot describe a job before the noun.`,2:`"Dangerously" is an adverb and cannot describe a noun.`,0:`"Endangered" describes species that are at risk.`}},{id:`b1-mc-405`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`What is the correct word for the official document that lets you enter another country for a limited time?`,explanation:`A visa gives permission to enter a country.`,options:[`visa`,`passport`,`ticket`,`licence`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`A passport identifies you.`,2:`A ticket lets you travel.`,3:`A licence allows you to do something like driving.`}},{id:`b1-mc-406`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`The superlative of a short adjective takes -est.`,options:[`She is the most young of the three sisters.`,`She is younger of the three sisters.`,`She is the youngest of the three sisters.`,`She is the youngest from the three sisters.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`Short adjectives take -est.`,1:`"Younger" compares only two.`,3:`"Of" is used with a group.`}},{id:`b1-mc-407`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`My new phone is not as ___ as my old one.`,explanation:`"As ... as" is used with the base form.`,options:[`reliability`,`more reliable`,`reliably`,`reliable`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`After "as ... as" we use the base adjective.`,2:`After "be" we need an adjective, not an adverb.`,0:`A noun does not fit after "as ... as".`}},{id:`b1-mc-408`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`I'm sorry, I can't hear you. Could you speak ___?`,explanation:`"Speak up" means to speak louder.`,options:[`up`,`on`,`in`,`out`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Speak on" means to continue speaking.`,3:`"Speak out" means to say publicly.`,2:`"Speak in" is not a phrasal verb.`}},{id:`b1-mc-409`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The film, ___ was made in 1999, is still very popular.`,explanation:`"Which" is used in a clause with commas for things.`,options:[`who`,`where`,`which`,`that`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"That" is not used in clauses with commas.`,0:`"Who" is used for people.`,1:`"Where" refers to a place.`}},{id:`b1-mc-410`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`The more you practise, the ___ you will become.`,explanation:`"The more ..., the better" is a comparative pattern.`,options:[`more good`,`better`,`best`,`good`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`The superlative does not fit "the more ... the".`,3:`The comparative is needed.`,0:`"Good" has an irregular comparative.`}},{id:`b1-mc-411`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`The company has made a big ___ in sales this year.`,explanation:`"Increase" is the noun.`,options:[`increased`,`increasing`,`increase`,`increasingly`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Increasing" is a participle.`,0:`"Increased" is a past form.`,3:`"Increasingly" is an adverb.`}},{id:`b1-mc-412`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Please ___ the document and send it back to me.`,explanation:`"Sign" means to write your name.`,options:[`sing`,`sink`,`sigh`,`sign`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Sing" is to make music.`,2:`"Sigh" is a breath of tiredness.`,1:`"Sink" is a kitchen basin.`}},{id:`b1-mc-413`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`The past continuous shows an action in progress.`,options:[`She is reading when I called.`,`She read when I called.`,`She has read when I called.`,`She was reading when I called.`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`The longer action needs the past continuous.`,2:`The present perfect does not fit "when I called".`,0:`The sentence is in the past.`}},{id:`b1-mc-414`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`She's very good ___ solving mathematical problems.`,explanation:`"Good at" is used for skills.`,options:[`on`,`in`,`with`,`at`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Good in" is not used for skills in this sense.`,0:`"Good on" has a different, informal meaning.`,2:`"Good with" refers to dealing with people or things.`}},{id:`b1-mc-415`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`There's ___ man at the door. He wants to talk to you.`,explanation:`"A" introduces someone new.`,options:[`–`,`an`,`the`,`a`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"The" would suggest we both know him.`,1:`"Man" begins with a consonant sound.`,0:`A singular countable noun needs an article.`}},{id:`b1-mc-416`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`This ticket is ___ for one month.`,explanation:`"Valid" means usable.`,options:[`useful`,`true`,`active`,`valid`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Useful" is not about time.`,2:`"Active" is not usual for tickets.`,1:`"True" is not used for tickets.`}},{id:`b1-mc-417`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Tom is ___ than his brother, so he often wins when they race.`,explanation:`The comparative of "quick" is "quicker".`,options:[`quickest`,`more quick`,`quicker`,`quick`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`A comparison with "than" needs a comparative form.`,0:`The superlative does not fit "than".`,1:`Short adjectives take -er.`}},{id:`b1-mc-418`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`What do we call a long walk in the mountains?`,explanation:`A hike is a long walk.`,options:[`hack`,`hike`,`hick`,`hook`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`This is not a word for walking.`,0:`"hack" does not work here, because this is not a word for walking.`,3:`"hook" does not work here, because this is not a word for walking.`}},{id:`b1-mc-419`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Which sentence uses the correct adjective order?`,explanation:`The order is opinion, colour, material.`,options:[`I bought a lovely red cotton shirt.`,`I bought a cotton red lovely shirt.`,`I bought a lovely cotton red shirt.`,`I bought a red lovely cotton shirt.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`Opinion should come first.`,3:`Opinion comes before colour.`,2:`Colour comes before material.`}},{id:`b1-mc-420`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`She spent hours ___ the phone.`,explanation:`"On the phone" is the fixed expression.`,options:[`in`,`at`,`on`,`by`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"In the phone" is not used.`,1:`"At the phone" is not the fixed phrase.`,3:`"By" shows a means or a doer.`}},{id:`b1-mc-421`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`I spoke to Anna and Peter, and I gave ___ the message.`,explanation:`"Them" is an object pronoun.`,options:[`their`,`theirs`,`they`,`them`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"They" is a subject pronoun.`,0:`"Their" is a possessive adjective.`,1:`"Theirs" is a possessive pronoun.`}},{id:`b1-mc-422`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`I'm good ___ languages, but I'm bad at maths.`,explanation:`"Good at" is used for skills.`,options:[`for`,`at`,`on`,`in`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Good in" is not used for skills.`,2:`"Good on" has another meaning.`,0:`"Good for" means beneficial.`}},{id:`b1-mc-423`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The local council wants to ___ more green spaces in the city.`,explanation:`"Create" means to make something new.`,options:[`create`,`pretend`,`discover`,`produce`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Produce" is used for goods.`,2:`"Discover" means to find.`,1:`"Pretend" does not fit.`}},{id:`b1-mc-424`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Is there ___ cheese in the fridge?`,explanation:`"Any" is used in questions.`,options:[`few`,`a`,`any`,`many`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"A" cannot be used with an uncountable noun.`,3:`"Many" is used with plural countable nouns.`,0:`"Few" is used with plural countable nouns.`}},{id:`b1-mc-425`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Each of the students ___ a different task.`,explanation:`"Each of" takes a singular verb.`,options:[`are having`,`have`,`having`,`has`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Each" takes a singular verb.`,0:`"are having" does not work here, because "Each" takes a singular verb.`,2:`A finite verb is needed.`}},{id:`b1-mc-426`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`He is ___ about his appearance. He always spends hours in front of the mirror.`,explanation:`"Vain" means too proud of one's looks.`,options:[`vain`,`vague`,`vein`,`vane`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`A vein is a blood vessel.`,3:`A vane is a weather instrument.`,1:`"Vague" means unclear.`}},{id:`b1-mc-427`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`That's the girl ___ brother plays for Real Madrid.`,explanation:`"Whose" shows possession in a relative clause.`,options:[`who`,`which`,`whose`,`whom`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Who" cannot show possession.`,1:`"Which" is used for things.`,3:`"Whom" is an object pronoun and does not show possession.`}},{id:`b1-mc-428`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`She works as a ___ in a hospital, taking care of patients' teeth.`,explanation:`A dentist cares for teeth.`,options:[`optician`,`chemist`,`surgeon`,`dentist`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`A chemist sells medicine.`,0:`An optician tests eyes.`,2:`A surgeon performs operations.`}},{id:`b1-mc-429`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`He plays the piano ___ than his sister.`,explanation:`The comparative of "well" is "better".`,options:[`good`,`best`,`better`,`well`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`A comparison with "than" needs a comparative form.`,1:`The superlative does not fit "than".`,3:`"Well" does not compare.`}},{id:`b1-mc-430`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`I completely agree ___ you about this.`,explanation:`"Agree with someone" expresses the same opinion.`,options:[`on`,`to`,`with`,`for`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Agree to" is used for plans or suggestions.`,0:`"Agree on" is used for a decision.`,3:`"Agree for" is not correct.`}},{id:`b1-mc-431`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`My neighbours are very noisy. I can't ___ with it any longer.`,explanation:`"Put up with" means tolerate.`,options:[`keep up`,`go on`,`put up`,`take up`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Go on" does not take "with it" like this.`,0:`"Keep up with" means to stay level.`,3:`"Take up" means to start.`}},{id:`b1-mc-432`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`She cut ___ with a knife while she was cooking.`,explanation:`"Herself" is used when subject and object are the same.`,options:[`her`,`herself`,`hers`,`she`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Her" would refer to another person.`,3:`"She" is a subject pronoun.`,2:`"Hers" is a possessive pronoun.`}},{id:`b1-mc-433`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The meeting was ___ because the manager was ill.`,explanation:`"Postponed" means moved to a later time.`,options:[`postponed`,`prevented`,`proposed`,`prepared`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Prepared" has a different meaning.`,1:`"Prevented" means stopped from happening.`,2:`"Proposed" means suggested.`}},{id:`b1-mc-434`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`We ___ a dog when I was a child.`,explanation:`"Used to have" describes a past state.`,options:[`use to have`,`used to have`,`were used to have`,`would to have`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`In the affirmative we need "used".`,2:`"Be used to" is followed by a gerund.`,3:`"Would" is not followed by "to".`}},{id:`b1-mc-435`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`She ___ have forgotten. She is always so careful.`,explanation:`"Can't have" is a negative deduction.`,options:[`can't`,`must`,`needn't`,`should`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Must have" would contradict "so careful".`,3:`"Should have" expresses regret.`,2:`"Needn't have" is about unnecessary actions.`}},{id:`b1-mc-436`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The new teacher ___ to the class yesterday.`,explanation:`"Was introduced" is the past passive.`,options:[`introduced`,`is introduced`,`was introduced`,`has introduced`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`The teacher was the person introduced.`,1:`"Yesterday" needs the past.`,3:`The teacher did not introduce.`}},{id:`b1-mc-437`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Dinner is almost ___. Please wash your hands.`,explanation:`"Ready" means prepared.`,options:[`done up`,`prepared up`,`made up`,`ready`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Prepared up" is not correct.`,2:`"Made up" means invented.`,0:`"Done up" means fastened.`}},{id:`b1-mc-438`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The injured man ___ to hospital by ambulance.`,explanation:`"Was taken" is the past passive.`,options:[`was taken`,`took`,`is taking`,`has taken`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`The man did not take anything.`,2:`The man is not the one taking.`,3:`The man did not take.`}},{id:`b1-mc-439`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`Can you ___ me a favour and look after my cat this weekend?`,explanation:`"Do someone a favour" is the standard collocation.`,options:[`take`,`give`,`make`,`do`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Make a favour" is not a standard collocation in English.`,1:`"Give a favour" is not the usual expression.`,0:`"Take a favour" means something different.`}},{id:`b1-mc-440`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`___ is raining, so take an umbrella.`,explanation:`"It" is used for weather.`,options:[`This`,`There`,`It`,`He`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"There" introduces the existence of something.`,0:`"This" is a demonstrative.`,3:`"He" refers to a male person.`}},{id:`b1-mc-441`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The government has introduced a ___ on smoking in public places.`,explanation:`"A ban on" means an official prohibition.`,options:[`order`,`ban`,`stop`,`block`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Order" means a command.`,3:`"Block" is not used this way.`,2:`"Stop" is not the noun used here.`}},{id:`b1-mc-442`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`I'm going to Madrid ___ my sister next weekend.`,explanation:`"With" shows accompaniment.`,options:[`and`,`together`,`by`,`with`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"By" is used for means of transport.`,0:`"And" would not fit after "go".`,1:`"Together" needs "with".`}},{id:`b1-mc-443`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`I don't know ___ man who lives next door.`,explanation:`"The" refers to a specific person.`,options:[`an`,`–`,`the`,`a`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"A" suggests any man, but we mean the one next door.`,0:`"Man" begins with a consonant sound.`,1:`A singular countable noun needs an article.`}},{id:`b1-mc-444`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`I love ___ chocolate, especially dark chocolate.`,explanation:`For general likes with uncountable nouns we use no article.`,options:[`–`,`a`,`some`,`the`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"The chocolate" would mean a specific chocolate, not chocolate in general.`,1:`"Chocolate" is uncountable here, so "a" does not fit.`,2:`"Some" would not express a general preference.`}},{id:`b1-mc-445`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`I was reading ___ I was waiting for the train.`,explanation:`"While" is followed by a clause.`,options:[`since`,`while`,`for`,`during`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"During" is followed by a noun, not a clause.`,2:`"For" is followed by a length of time.`,0:`"Since" starts at a point in the past.`}},{id:`b1-mc-446`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`I looked everywhere, but I couldn't find my keys ___.`,explanation:`"Anywhere" is used after a negative verb.`,options:[`anywhere`,`somewhere`,`everywhere`,`nowhere`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Somewhere" is used in positive sentences.`,3:`"Nowhere" is already negative.`,2:`"Everywhere" means all places.`}},{id:`b1-mc-447`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`He was ___ because the company closed the factory.`,explanation:`"Made redundant" means losing a job because it is no longer needed.`,options:[`retired`,`quit`,`fired`,`made redundant`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Fired" suggests a dismissal for misconduct.`,1:`"Quit" means he left by choice.`,0:`"Retired" means stopping work due to age.`}},{id:`b1-mc-448`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`My sister lives in ___ United States.`,explanation:`Countries with plural or "Republic/Kingdom" names take "the".`,options:[`–`,`the`,`an`,`a`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"United States" is a plural name and takes "the".`,3:`"A" does not go with a country's name.`,2:`"An" does not go with a country's name.`}},{id:`b1-mc-449`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"Arrive at" is used with places.`,options:[`We arrived in the airport at six.`,`We arrived to the airport at six.`,`We arrived on the airport at six.`,`We arrived at the airport at six.`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Arrive" is not followed by "to".`,2:`"On" does not fit with "arrive".`,0:`"In" suggests inside the building.`}},{id:`b1-mc-450`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"Accused of" + gerund.`,options:[`She was accused for stealing the money.`,`She was accused to steal the money.`,`She accused of stealing the money.`,`She was accused of stealing the money.`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Accused of" is followed by a gerund.`,0:`"Accused for" is not correct.`,2:`The passive needs "was".`}},{id:`b1-mc-451`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Which word describes someone who owns a business?`,explanation:`An entrepreneur starts and runs a business.`,options:[`employee`,`entrepreneur`,`customer`,`colleague`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`An employee works for a business.`,2:`A customer buys from a business.`,3:`A colleague works with you.`}},{id:`b1-mc-452`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`I wish I ___ so much homework tonight!`,explanation:`"I wish I didn't have" expresses a present regret.`,options:[`hadn't had`,`didn't have`,`don't have`,`won't have`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`After "wish" the present moves to the past.`,3:`"Won't" is not used after "wish".`,0:`This refers to the past.`}},{id:`b1-mc-453`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`I need to ___ my English before the interview.`,explanation:`"Improve" means to make better.`,options:[`increase`,`rise`,`raise`,`improve`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`It is used for amounts.`,2:`It is used for lifting.`,1:`It does not take an object.`}},{id:`b1-mc-454`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Teachers ___ a lot of homework at the end of the term.`,explanation:`"Set homework" is the standard collocation.`,options:[`give out`,`put`,`set`,`make`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Put homework" is not used.`,0:`"Give out" means to distribute.`,3:`"Make homework" is not used.`}},{id:`b1-mc-455`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`He was ___ of stealing a car.`,explanation:`"Accused of" is the correct collocation.`,options:[`accused`,`blamed`,`arrested`,`charged`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Blamed" does not take "of stealing" like this.`,3:`"Charged" takes "with".`,2:`"Arrested" takes "for".`}},{id:`b1-mc-456`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`My sister, ___ lives in Canada, is visiting us next month.`,explanation:`"Who" is used in a clause with commas for people.`,options:[`that`,`which`,`who`,`whose`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"That" is not used in clauses with commas.`,1:`"Which" is used for things.`,3:`"Whose" shows possession.`}},{id:`b1-mc-457`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The bus stop is ___ the post office and the bank.`,explanation:`"Between" is used for two things.`,options:[`between`,`among`,`beside`,`behind`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Among" is used for more than two things.`,2:`"Beside" means next to one thing.`,3:`"Behind" means at the back.`}},{id:`b1-mc-458`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`She is the ___ student in our class.`,explanation:`Long adjectives form the superlative with "most".`,options:[`more intelligent`,`most intelligent`,`intelligentest`,`the intelligent`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"More" is used to compare only two people.`,2:`Long adjectives do not take -est.`,3:`This lacks the superlative form.`}},{id:`b1-mc-459`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Doctors recommend a ___ diet with plenty of fruit and vegetables.`,explanation:`"Balanced diet" is the usual collocation.`,options:[`even`,`balanced`,`equal`,`fair`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Equal" does not collocate with diet.`,0:`"Even" does not collocate with diet.`,3:`"Fair" does not collocate with diet.`}},{id:`b1-mc-460`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`This exercise is ___ than the one we did yesterday.`,explanation:`Longer adjectives form the comparative with "more".`,options:[`more difficult`,`difficulter`,`most difficult`,`the more difficult`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`Long adjectives do not take -er; they take "more".`,2:`"Most" forms the superlative, not the comparative.`,3:`"The" does not fit a simple comparison here.`}},{id:`b1-mc-461`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Which word describes a person who is travelling on a ship or plane?`,explanation:`A passenger travels in a vehicle without driving.`,options:[`tourist`,`pedestrian`,`passenger`,`driver`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`A pedestrian walks on the street.`,3:`A driver operates a vehicle.`,0:`A tourist visits places for pleasure.`}},{id:`b1-mc-462`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"So ... that" expresses a result.`,options:[`It was very cold that we stayed inside.`,`It was so cold that we stayed inside.`,`It was too cold that we stayed inside.`,`It was such cold that we stayed inside.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Such" needs a noun phrase.`,2:`"Too" is not followed by "that".`,0:`"Very" does not express a result.`}},{id:`b1-mc-463`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`We're looking forward ___ seeing you at the party.`,explanation:`"Look forward to" is followed by -ing.`,options:[`at`,`for`,`on`,`to`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Look forward for" is not correct.`,0:`"Look forward at" is not correct.`,2:`"Look forward on" is not correct.`}},{id:`b1-mc-464`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`We use "the" with musical instruments.`,options:[`She plays a piano very well.`,`She plays the piano very well.`,`She plays an piano very well.`,`She plays piano very well.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`With instruments we normally use "the".`,0:`"A piano" does not describe playing the instrument.`,2:`"Piano" begins with a consonant sound.`}},{id:`b1-mc-465`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"Good at" is used for skills.`,options:[`He is good at solving problems.`,`He is good for solving problems.`,`He is good in solving problems.`,`He is good on solving problems.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Good in" is not used for skills.`,3:`"Good on" has a different meaning.`,1:`"Good for" means beneficial.`}},{id:`b1-mc-466`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`My flatmate is very ___. He always leaves dirty dishes in the sink.`,explanation:`"Untidy" describes messy behaviour.`,options:[`mean`,`untidy`,`tidy`,`strict`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Tidy" is the opposite.`,3:`"Strict" refers to rules.`,0:`"Mean" refers to unkindness or stinginess.`}},{id:`b1-mc-467`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`I have to ___ a lot of homework tonight, so I can't go out.`,explanation:`"Do homework" is the standard collocation.`,options:[`make`,`take`,`have to`,`do`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Make homework" is not a standard collocation.`,1:`"Take homework" does not mean to work on it.`,2:`"Have to" would repeat the first modal.`}},{id:`b1-mc-468`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`He was taken to ___ hospital after the accident.`,explanation:`In British English there is no article in "to hospital" for patients.`,options:[`an`,`–`,`the`,`a`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`In British English we say "to hospital" when we mean as a patient.`,3:`"A hospital" suggests any building, not the patient meaning.`,0:`"Hospital" begins with a consonant sound.`}},{id:`b1-mc-469`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The software ___ automatically every night.`,explanation:`"Updates" installs recent changes.`,options:[`uploads`,`unlocks`,`upgrades`,`updates`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Upgrades" means improves to a higher version.`,0:`"Uploads" sends data to the internet.`,1:`"Unlocks" opens something.`}},{id:`b1-mc-470`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`___ Mount Fuji is in Japan.`,explanation:`Single mountain names take no article.`,options:[`–`,`The`,`A`,`An`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`A single mountain name takes no article.`,2:`"A" does not fit a proper name.`,3:`"An" does not fit a proper name.`}},{id:`b1-mc-471`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"Despite" is followed directly by a noun.`,options:[`Despite the rain, we went out.`,`Despite of the rain, we went out.`,`Although the rain, we went out.`,`In spite the rain, we went out.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Despite" is not followed by "of".`,2:`"Although" needs a clause.`,3:`"In spite" needs "of".`}},{id:`b1-mc-472`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Hospitals face a ___ of nurses.`,explanation:`"A shortage of" means not enough.`,options:[`little`,`absence`,`lack of`,`shortage`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Lack of" would require another structure.`,0:`"Little" does not fit with "of nurses".`,1:`"Absence" means not being present.`}},{id:`b1-mc-473`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`He ___ his passport at the airport and had to wait for a new one.`,explanation:`"Lost" is the past simple of "lose".`,options:[`loose`,`lose`,`loosed`,`lost`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Loosed" means released.`,1:`The sentence is in the past.`,0:`"Loose" means not tight.`}},{id:`b1-mc-474`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`I'm tired ___ listening to the same music every day.`,explanation:`"Tired of" means bored with something.`,options:[`with`,`of`,`from`,`at`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Tired from" is used after physical effort.`,0:`"Tired with" is not the normal combination here.`,3:`"Tired at" is not correct.`}},{id:`b1-mc-475`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Everest is ___ highest mountain in the world.`,explanation:`Superlatives take "the".`,options:[`an`,`–`,`the`,`a`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"A" cannot go with a superlative.`,1:`A superlative needs "the".`,0:`"An" cannot go with a superlative.`}},{id:`b1-mc-476`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`We should reduce the amount of plastic we ___.`,explanation:`"Use" fits plastic.`,options:[`waste`,`use`,`spend`,`consume`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Consume" is used mainly for food and energy.`,0:`"Waste" has a negative meaning in this sentence.`,2:`"Spend" is used with money or time.`}},{id:`b1-mc-477`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,topics:[`phrasal-collocations-idioms`],prompt:`We had to ___ the picnic because it started to rain.`,explanation:`"Call off" means to cancel.`,options:[`call in`,`call on`,`call up`,`call off`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Call up" means to telephone.`,0:`"Call in" means to ask someone to come.`,1:`"Call on" means to visit.`}},{id:`b1-mc-478`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The new employee found it difficult to ___ in at first.`,explanation:`"Fit in" means to be accepted by a group.`,options:[`fit`,`fix`,`fill`,`feel`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Fix" means to repair.`,2:`"Fill" means to make full.`,3:`"Feel in" is not a phrasal verb.`}},{id:`b1-mc-479`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"The children" refers to specific children.`,options:[`Children are playing in the garden the.`,`The childrens are playing in the garden.`,`A children are playing in the garden.`,`The children are playing in the garden.`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`The word order is incorrect.`,2:`"A" cannot go with a plural noun.`,1:`"Children" is already plural.`}},{id:`b1-mc-480`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`___ Nile is the longest river in Africa.`,explanation:`Names of rivers take "the".`,options:[`An`,`A`,`–`,`The`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`There is only one Nile, so "a" is wrong.`,2:`Names of rivers take "the".`,0:`"An" does not fit a river name.`}},{id:`b1-mc-481`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"Depend on" is the fixed combination.`,options:[`He depends of his parents for money.`,`He depends in his parents for money.`,`He depends on his parents for money.`,`He depends from his parents for money.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Depend of" is not correct.`,3:`"Depend from" is not correct.`,1:`"Depend in" is not correct.`}},{id:`b1-mc-482`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Do you have ___ sugar in your tea?`,explanation:`"Any" is used in questions.`,options:[`few`,`an`,`many`,`any`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"An" cannot be used with an uncountable noun.`,0:`"Few" is used with plural nouns.`,2:`"Many" is used with plural nouns.`}},{id:`b1-mc-483`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"Interested in" is followed by a gerund.`,options:[`We are interested of learning Italian.`,`We are interested in learning Italian.`,`We are interested to learn Italian.`,`We are interested on learning Italian.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Interested" is followed by "in" and a gerund.`,3:`"Interested on" is not correct.`,0:`"Interested of" is not correct.`}},{id:`b1-mc-484`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The cat was hiding ___ the sofa.`,explanation:`"Behind" means at the back of.`,options:[`among`,`against`,`behind`,`beside`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Against" means touching in opposition.`,0:`"Among" needs several things.`,3:`"Beside" means next to.`}},{id:`b1-mc-485`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Is the price ___ of breakfast?`,explanation:`"Inclusive of" means that something is included.`,options:[`including`,`exclusive`,`inclusive`,`included`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Including" does not take "of".`,3:`"Included" is used without "of".`,1:`It means the opposite.`}},{id:`b1-mc-486`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`We need to buy ___ new fridge. The old one is broken.`,explanation:`"A" introduces something new.`,options:[`the`,`–`,`an`,`a`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"New" begins with a consonant sound.`,0:`"The" would mean a fridge already mentioned.`,1:`A singular countable noun needs an article.`}},{id:`b1-mc-487`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`I've got ___ to tell you. It's very important.`,explanation:`"Something" is used in positive sentences.`,options:[`nothing`,`something`,`everything`,`anything`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Anything" is used in questions and negatives.`,0:`"Nothing" is negative.`,2:`"Everything" means all things.`}},{id:`b1-mc-488`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`He looked ___ when he heard the news.`,explanation:`After "look" we use an adjective.`,options:[`worriedly`,`worried`,`worry`,`worrying`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`This is a verb or noun.`,3:`It describes what causes worry.`,0:`This is not the usual adverb.`}},{id:`b1-mc-489`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`The test was ___ difficult that nobody passed.`,explanation:`"So ... that" expresses a result.`,options:[`such`,`very`,`so`,`too`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Such" is followed by a noun phrase.`,3:`"Too" does not introduce a result.`,1:`"Very" is not followed by "that".`}},{id:`b1-mc-490`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`There isn't ___ milk left in the fridge.`,explanation:`"Any" is used in negative sentences.`,options:[`many`,`any`,`a`,`few`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"A" cannot be used with an uncountable noun.`,0:`"Many" is used with plural countable nouns.`,3:`"Few" is used with plural countable nouns.`}},{id:`b1-mc-491`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`___ of the two boys was able to answer the question.`,explanation:`"Neither" is used for two people with a negative meaning.`,options:[`Every`,`Both`,`None`,`Neither`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"None" is used for more than two.`,1:`"Both" would mean they both answered.`,0:`"Every" is followed by a singular noun.`}},{id:`b1-mc-492`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`What do we call the money you pay to travel on a bus or train?`,explanation:`A fare is the price of a journey.`,options:[`fare`,`fine`,`fee`,`tip`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`A fine is a punishment payment.`,2:`A fee is a payment for a service.`,3:`A tip is extra money for service.`}},{id:`b1-mc-493`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`He was punished ___ breaking the rules.`,explanation:`"Punish for" gives the reason.`,options:[`with`,`of`,`by`,`for`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Punished of" is not correct.`,2:`"By" introduces the person who punishes.`,0:`"With" introduces an instrument.`}},{id:`b1-mc-494`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`My cousin works as ___ nurse in a children's hospital.`,explanation:`"A" is used with jobs.`,options:[`a`,`–`,`the`,`an`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Nurse" begins with a consonant sound.`,2:`"The" would mean a particular nurse.`,1:`With jobs we need an article.`}},{id:`b1-mc-495`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`What time does ___ film start?`,explanation:`"The film" refers to a specific, known film.`,options:[`an`,`a`,`–`,`the`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"A film" would mean any film, but both speakers know which one.`,0:`"Film" begins with a consonant sound.`,2:`A singular noun needs an article.`}},{id:`b1-mc-496`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The conference will take place ___ the second week of June.`,explanation:`"In" is used with weeks and months.`,options:[`at`,`on`,`during on`,`in`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"On" is used with days or dates.`,0:`"At" is used with clock times and short points.`,2:`This is not a correct structure.`}},{id:`b1-mc-497`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`He drives ___ than his brother, so he has had fewer accidents.`,explanation:`Adverbs with -ly form the comparative with "more".`,options:[`more carefully`,`more careful`,`most carefully`,`carefuller`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`Adverbs ending in -ly use "more", not -er.`,2:`"Most" forms the superlative.`,1:`A verb needs an adverb, not an adjective.`}},{id:`b1-mc-498`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`He built the whole house ___.`,explanation:`"Himself" emphasises that he did it alone.`,options:[`his`,`him`,`he`,`himself`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Him" is an object pronoun and does not express "without help".`,0:`"His" is a possessive adjective.`,2:`"He" is a subject pronoun.`}},{id:`b1-mc-499`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`He is responsible ___ organising the event.`,explanation:`"Responsible for" is followed by -ing or a noun.`,options:[`to`,`of`,`for`,`about`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Responsible of" is not correct.`,0:`"Responsible to" is followed by a person.`,3:`"Responsible about" is not correct.`}},{id:`b1-mc-500`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Would you like ___ more tea?`,explanation:`"Some" is used in offers.`,options:[`many`,`some`,`any`,`a`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Any" is used in questions only when no offer is meant.`,3:`"A" cannot be used with an uncountable noun.`,0:`"Many" is used with plural countable nouns.`}},{id:`b1-mc-501`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`Which sentence is a correct reported question?`,explanation:`Reported questions use statement word order.`,options:[`He asked me where I lived.`,`He asked me where do I live.`,`He asked me where did I live.`,`He asked me where I live now.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`A reported question does not use "did".`,1:`A reported question does not invert subject and verb.`,3:`This could be correct but changes the time reference in this exercise.`}},{id:`b1-mc-502`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`The present perfect passive is "has been postponed".`,options:[`The meeting is postpone until Monday.`,`The meeting has been postponing until Monday.`,`The meeting has postponed until Monday.`,`The meeting has been postponed until Monday.`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`A meeting cannot postpone itself.`,0:`The passive needs a participle.`,1:`"The meeting has been postponing until Monday." does not work here, because the passive needs a participle.`}},{id:`b1-mc-503`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`We'd like to ___ you to our wedding.`,explanation:`"Invite" means to ask someone to come.`,options:[`ask up`,`welcome`,`call in`,`invite`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Ask up" is not a phrasal verb.`,2:`"Call in" has another meaning.`,1:`"Welcome" is what you do when guests arrive.`}},{id:`b1-mc-504`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Please ___ your shoes at the door.`,explanation:`"Take off" means to remove clothes.`,options:[`give off`,`put off`,`turn off`,`take off`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Turn off" is for machines.`,0:`"Give off" means to produce.`,1:`"Put off" means to delay.`}},{id:`b1-mc-505`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`___ of the three boys is a good dancer.`,explanation:`"None" is used for more than two.`,options:[`No`,`Neither`,`Nobody`,`None`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Neither" is used for two people.`,2:`"Nobody" cannot be followed by "of".`,0:`"No" cannot be followed by "of".`}},{id:`b1-mc-506`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`The exam was easier ___ I expected.`,explanation:`"Than" follows a comparative.`,options:[`as`,`that`,`than`,`from`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"As" is used for equality.`,1:`"That" does not form a comparison.`,3:`"From" does not form a comparison.`}},{id:`b1-mc-507`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`The weather is going to be ___ tomorrow. Take your umbrella.`,explanation:`"Wet" means rainy.`,options:[`wet`,`humid`,`soaked`,`watery`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Humid" means damp air without rain.`,2:`"Soaked" means extremely wet.`,3:`"Watery" means like water.`}},{id:`b1-mc-508`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`We usually have a big dinner ___ Christmas Eve.`,explanation:`"On" is used with a specific day.`,options:[`by`,`at`,`in`,`on`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"At" is used with the Christmas period as a whole.`,2:`"In" is used with months.`,0:`"By" shows a deadline.`}},{id:`b1-mc-509`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`Which reporting verb fits? He ___ me not to be late.`,explanation:`"Warn someone not to".`,options:[`warned`,`told that`,`said`,`spoke`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Said" is not followed by an object and "not to".`,1:`This needs a person after "told".`,3:`"Speak" does not fit.`}},{id:`b1-mc-510`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`He's allergic ___ nuts, so he never eats chocolate.`,explanation:`"Allergic to" is the fixed collocation.`,options:[`at`,`of`,`with`,`to`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Allergic with" is not correct.`,1:`"Allergic of" is not correct.`,0:`"Allergic at" is not correct.`}},{id:`b1-mc-511`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`I find it difficult to ___ my time well when I have so much work.`,explanation:`"Manage your time" is the standard collocation.`,options:[`control up`,`handle up`,`manage`,`direct`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Handle up" is not used.`,0:`"Control up" is not used.`,3:`"Direct" does not collocate with time.`}},{id:`b1-mc-512`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The doctor told me to ___ more water.`,explanation:`"Drink" is the verb for liquids.`,options:[`have to`,`take`,`eat`,`drink`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`You eat food but drink liquids.`,1:`"Take" is used with medicine, not water in this sense.`,0:`"Have to" needs a verb after it.`}},{id:`b1-mc-513`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The photos are too big to send. You need to ___ them.`,explanation:`"Compress" means to make a file smaller.`,options:[`express`,`impress`,`compress`,`depress`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Express" means to show feelings.`,3:`"Depress" means to make sad.`,1:`"Impress" means to make a good impression.`}},{id:`b1-mc-514`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`I bought a jacket and a shirt. ___ jacket was expensive, but the shirt was cheap.`,explanation:`"The" refers to something already mentioned.`,options:[`–`,`A`,`The`,`An`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"A" would introduce a different jacket.`,3:`"An" cannot be used before "jacket".`,0:`A singular noun needs an article.`}},{id:`b1-mc-515`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`We had ___ wonderful time at the party.`,explanation:`"Have a wonderful time" is a fixed expression with "a".`,options:[`–`,`an`,`the`,`a`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"The" would refer to a time already identified.`,1:`"Wonderful" begins with a consonant sound, so "an" is wrong.`,0:`A singular noun phrase like this needs an article.`}},{id:`b1-mc-516`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`I'd like to go to ___ university to study medicine.`,explanation:`When we talk about the purpose of the place, we use no article: go to university.`,options:[`a`,`an`,`the`,`–`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"The university" would mean one particular building, not studying there.`,0:`"A university" would mean any single university building.`,1:`"University" begins with the sound /j/, so "an" is wrong.`}},{id:`b1-mc-517`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Our flight was ___ for two hours because of fog.`,explanation:`"Delayed" means late.`,options:[`cancelled`,`delayed`,`stopped`,`postponed`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Cancelled" means it did not happen.`,3:`"Postponed" is used for events.`,2:`"Stopped" does not collocate with flights.`}},{id:`b1-mc-518`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`He's an excellent ___: he can fix anything in the house.`,explanation:`A handyman is a person who does repairs.`,options:[`handyman`,`handbag`,`handout`,`handwriting`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Handwriting" means the way a person writes.`,1:`A handbag is something women carry.`,2:`A handout is a paper given in class.`}},{id:`b1-mc-519`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Which sentence expresses a regret about the past?`,explanation:`"Wish + past perfect" expresses a past regret.`,options:[`I wish I studied more.`,`I wish I will study more.`,`I wish I had studied more.`,`I wish I study more.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`This is a present wish.`,1:`"Will" is not used after "wish".`,3:`This does not use a past form.`}},{id:`b1-mc-520`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`"He can't have seen us." This means:`,explanation:`"Can't have" + past participle = impossible in the past.`,options:[`He didn't want to see us.`,`He isn't allowed to see us.`,`He will not see us.`,`I'm sure he didn't see us.`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`It is a deduction, not a wish.`,1:`It is not about permission.`,2:`It is not about the future.`}},{id:`b1-mc-521`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`I'm afraid I can't come. I'm tied ___ with work.`,explanation:`"Tied up" means very busy.`,options:[`up`,`down`,`off`,`out`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Tied out" is not a phrasal verb.`,1:`"Tied down" has a different meaning.`,2:`"Tied off" is not used.`}},{id:`b1-mc-522`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The company will ___ twenty new staff this year.`,explanation:`"Recruit" means to find new employees.`,options:[`rescue`,`recruit`,`reserve`,`resign`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Rescue" means to save.`,2:`"Reserve" means to book.`,3:`"Resign" means to leave a job.`}},{id:`b1-mc-523`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Neither of my brothers ___ married.`,explanation:`"Neither of" takes a singular verb.`,options:[`have`,`were`,`are`,`is`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Neither of" is usually followed by a singular verb.`,1:`The sentence is in the present.`,0:`"Have married" is not a correct form here.`}},{id:`b1-mc-524`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The new law aims to ___ pollution from cars.`,explanation:`"Reduce" takes an object and means to make less.`,options:[`decrease`,`rise`,`raise`,`reduce`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Rise" does not take an object.`,2:`"Raise" increases, which is the opposite.`,0:`"Decrease" is usually intransitive in this sense.`}},{id:`b1-mc-525`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Which of these is NOT a stage of education?`,explanation:`A colleague is a person you work with.`,options:[`primary school`,`colleague`,`secondary school`,`university`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`This is a stage of education.`,2:`"secondary school" does not work here, because this is a stage of education.`,3:`"university" does not work here, because this is a stage of education.`}},{id:`b1-mc-526`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The book was written ___ a famous Spanish author.`,explanation:`"By" introduces the doer in a passive sentence.`,options:[`of`,`by`,`with`,`from`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"From" does not introduce the author.`,0:`"Of" does not introduce the author.`,2:`"With" introduces an instrument.`}},{id:`b1-mc-527`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"Since" calls for the present perfect.`,options:[`I am living here since 2015.`,`I lived here since 2015.`,`I live here since 2015.`,`I have lived here since 2015.`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Since" needs the present perfect.`,0:`"I am living here since 2015." does not work here, because "Since" needs the present perfect.`,1:`"I lived here since 2015." does not work here, because "Since" needs the present perfect.`}},{id:`b1-mc-528`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`What do we call a person who studies at university?`,explanation:`A student studies at university.`,options:[`pupil`,`apprentice`,`trainee`,`student`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Pupil" is used for school children.`,1:`An apprentice learns a trade at work.`,2:`A trainee learns a job.`}},{id:`b1-mc-529`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`It was ___ unusual situation, so nobody knew what to do.`,explanation:`"An" is used before a vowel sound.`,options:[`an`,`a`,`the`,`–`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Unusual" begins with a vowel sound.`,2:`"The" would suggest a situation already known.`,3:`A singular countable noun needs an article.`}},{id:`b1-mc-530`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If you ___ me, I'd tell you the truth.`,explanation:`"If you asked" is the unreal past form.`,options:[`asked`,`will ask`,`ask`,`had asked`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`The second conditional needs the past simple.`,1:`"Will" is not used in the if-clause.`,3:`This would need "would have told".`}},{id:`b1-mc-531`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`What do you call a short holiday of a few days in a city?`,explanation:`A city break is a short city holiday.`,options:[`day trip`,`city break`,`gap year`,`package tour`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`A day trip is only one day.`,3:`A package tour is a fully organised holiday.`,2:`A gap year is a long break.`}},{id:`b1-mc-532`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`We usually go to my aunt's house ___ Christmas.`,explanation:`"At Christmas" refers to the holiday period.`,options:[`at`,`on`,`by`,`in`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"On Christmas" is used only for the day itself in this context.`,3:`"In Christmas" is not correct.`,2:`"By" shows a deadline.`}},{id:`b1-mc-533`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`She's afraid ___ flying, so she travels by train.`,explanation:`"Afraid of" is followed by a noun or -ing.`,options:[`at`,`of`,`from`,`to`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Afraid from" is not correct.`,0:`"Afraid at" is not correct.`,3:`"Afraid to" is followed by a verb.`}},{id:`b1-mc-534`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`He has ___ little patience; he gets angry very quickly.`,explanation:`"Very little" emphasises the lack.`,options:[`a`,`very`,`so`,`the`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"A little" would be positive.`,3:`"The little" does not fit.`,2:`"So little" needs another structure.`}},{id:`b1-mc-535`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`I'm looking forward ___ you.`,explanation:`"Look forward to" + gerund.`,options:[`meet`,`meeting`,`to meet`,`to meeting`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"To" is a preposition here.`,1:`"To" is missing.`,0:`The base form does not follow "look forward to".`}},{id:`b1-mc-536`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`The ___ of living in this city has risen sharply in recent years.`,explanation:`"Cost of living" is the fixed expression.`,options:[`price`,`cost`,`charge`,`value`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Price" refers to a single item, not to general living expenses.`,3:`"Value" refers to how much something is worth.`,2:`"Charge" is an amount asked for a service.`}},{id:`b1-mc-537`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`I didn't understand ___ he said.`,explanation:`"What" means "the thing that".`,options:[`which`,`that`,`what`,`who`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"That" cannot replace "what" meaning "the things".`,0:`"Which" needs an antecedent.`,3:`"Who" refers to a person.`}},{id:`b1-mc-538`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Which word means "a place where you wait for a train"?`,explanation:`A platform is next to the train tracks.`,options:[`pavement`,`passage`,`platform`,`podium`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`A pavement is for walkers.`,1:`A passage is a narrow way.`,3:`A podium is a raised stand.`}},{id:`b1-mc-539`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`The water isn't warm ___ to swim in.`,explanation:`"Enough" goes after the adjective.`,options:[`much`,`enough`,`very`,`too`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Too" goes before the adjective.`,2:`"Very" goes before the adjective.`,0:`"Much" is not used after an adjective.`}},{id:`b1-mc-540`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`He got a ___ in maths because he was always bad at it.`,explanation:`"A low mark" is the usual collocation.`,options:[`short mark`,`low note`,`low mark`,`small mark`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Note" does not mean mark in this sense.`,3:`"Small" does not collocate.`,0:`"Short" does not collocate.`}},{id:`b1-mc-541`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`I forgot ___ umbrella this morning and got wet.`,explanation:`"My" shows that the umbrella belongs to the speaker.`,options:[`my`,`the`,`an`,`a`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"The" does not show ownership.`,2:`"An" does not show ownership.`,3:`"A" does not show ownership.`}},{id:`b1-mc-542`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`I can't ___ why he left without saying a word.`,explanation:`"Understand" means to grasp the meaning.`,options:[`undergo`,`stand`,`undertake`,`understand`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Stand" means to tolerate or to be upright.`,2:`"Undertake" means to begin a task.`,0:`"Undergo" means to experience a treatment.`}},{id:`b1-mc-543`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"By" introduces the doer in a passive sentence.`,options:[`The book was written from a young author.`,`The book was written with a young author.`,`The book was written by a young author.`,`The book was written of a young author.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"From" does not introduce the author.`,3:`"Of" does not introduce the author.`,1:`"With" introduces an instrument.`}},{id:`b1-mc-544`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`There were ___ people at the concert, so it was almost empty.`,explanation:`"Few" is used with plural countable nouns.`,options:[`a little`,`much`,`little`,`few`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Little" is used with uncountable nouns.`,1:`"Much" is used with uncountable nouns.`,0:`"A little" is used with uncountable nouns.`}},{id:`b1-mc-545`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`We've lived here ___ more than ten years.`,explanation:`"For" is followed by a period of time.`,options:[`in`,`since`,`for`,`during`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Since" is followed by a point in time.`,3:`"During" is followed by an event.`,0:`"In" does not show duration up to now.`}},{id:`b1-mc-546`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`If you want to save money, travel in the ___ season.`,explanation:`"Low season" is the standard collocation.`,options:[`little`,`cheap`,`small`,`low`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Cheap season" is not used.`,0:`"Little season" is not used.`,2:`"Small season" is not used.`}},{id:`b1-mc-547`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"Who" refers to people.`,options:[`The girl what lives next door is a singer.`,`The girl whose lives next door is a singer.`,`The girl who lives next door is a singer.`,`The girl which lives next door is a singer.`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Which" is not used for people.`,1:`"Whose" needs a noun after it.`,0:`"What" is not a relative pronoun here.`}},{id:`b1-mc-548`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"For" with duration up to now uses the present perfect.`,options:[`She is working here since ten years.`,`She has worked here for ten years.`,`She worked here since ten years.`,`She works here for ten years.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"For ten years" up to now needs a perfect form.`,2:`"Since" is followed by a point in time.`,0:`"She is working here since ten years." does not work here, because "Since" is followed by a point in time.`}},{id:`b1-mc-549`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`We ___ the match if the weather had been better.`,explanation:`"Would have played" is the third conditional result.`,options:[`would have played`,`had played`,`will play`,`would play`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`A third conditional result needs "would have".`,2:`"Will" does not fit an unreal past.`,1:`"Had played" is the if-clause form.`}},{id:`b1-mc-550`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`"Enjoy" + gerund.`,options:[`She enjoys swim in the sea.`,`She enjoys swimming in the sea.`,`She enjoys to swim in the sea.`,`She enjoys swam in the sea.`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Enjoy" is followed by a gerund.`,0:`The base form does not follow "enjoy".`,3:`The past form does not follow "enjoy".`}},{id:`b1-mc-551`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`She plays ___ violin in the school orchestra.`,explanation:`We use "the" with musical instruments: play the violin.`,options:[`the`,`an`,`–`,`a`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"A" would suggest one random violin, but with musical instruments we normally use "the".`,1:`"An" is used before vowel sounds, and "violin" begins with a consonant.`,2:`Without an article the phrase does not sound natural for instruments.`}},{id:`b1-mc-552`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`He runs ___ than I do, so he usually wins.`,explanation:`"Fast" is both an adjective and an adverb.`,options:[`more fast`,`fastly`,`fastest`,`faster`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`Short adverbs take -er.`,2:`The superlative does not fit "than".`,1:`"Fast" is the adverb and has no -ly form.`}},{id:`b1-mc-553`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I ___ you a postcard as soon as I arrive.`,explanation:`"Will" is used for a promise.`,options:[`am send`,`send`,`sent`,`will send`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`The main clause needs "will".`,2:`The sentence refers to the future.`,0:`This is not grammatical.`}},{id:`b1-mc-554`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`The doctor said I should ___ the medicine for a week.`,explanation:`"Take medicine" is the standard collocation.`,options:[`do`,`have up`,`eat`,`take`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Do medicine" is not used.`,1:`"Have up" is not a phrasal verb.`,2:`"Eat medicine" is not usual.`}},{id:`b1-mc-555`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`My parents don't approve ___ my decision to move abroad.`,explanation:`"Approve of" is the fixed expression.`,options:[`for`,`of`,`with`,`about`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Approve for" is not correct.`,2:`"Approve with" is not correct.`,3:`"Approve about" is not the standard combination.`}},{id:`b1-mc-556`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The tour bus ___ at the main square at ten o'clock.`,explanation:`"Departs" means leaves.`,options:[`leaves for`,`goes up`,`reaches`,`departs`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Reaches" does not fit the starting point.`,0:`"Leaves for" needs a destination.`,1:`"Goes up" has a different meaning.`}},{id:`b1-mc-557`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`She spends too much time ___ her phone.`,explanation:`"Spend time on".`,options:[`at`,`in`,`on`,`by`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"At the phone" means near it.`,3:`"By" is not used here.`,1:`"In" is not used here.`}},{id:`b1-mc-558`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Which sentence is a first conditional?`,explanation:`The first conditional is "if" + present simple + "will".`,options:[`If it rained, we would cancel the match.`,`If it had rained, we would have cancelled the match.`,`If it rains, we cancelled the match.`,`If it rains, we'll cancel the match.`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`This is a second conditional about an imaginary situation.`,1:`This is a third conditional about the past.`,2:`This mixes present and past.`}},{id:`b1-mc-559`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,explanation:`The comparative adverb uses "more".`,options:[`He drives more carefully than I do.`,`He drives most carefully than I do.`,`He drives more careful than I do.`,`He drives carefuller than I do.`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`A verb needs an adverb.`,3:`Long adverbs take "more".`,1:`"Most" is a superlative.`}},{id:`b1-mc-560`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`They arrived early ___ get good seats at the theatre.`,explanation:`"To" followed by a verb expresses purpose.`,options:[`so`,`in order`,`for`,`to`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"For" is followed by a noun or a gerund.`,0:`"So" needs "that" to express purpose.`,1:`"In order" needs "to" after it.`}}]};export{e as default};